{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"reasoning","text":"The count table is a 10kb binned genome-wide matrix: chrom, start, end + 15 organs (Bladder, Brain, Cloaca, GallBladder, Gill, Heart, Intestine, Kidney, Limb, Liver, Lung, Pancreas, Prostate, Spleen, Stomach).\n\nTask: Determine whether two organ labels are swapped. That is, the accessibility profile of some organ X looks like that of organ Y, and vice versa.\n\nStrategy ideas:\n1. Whole-genome bin correlation: if two labels are swapped, sample X will correlate better with the typical profile of organ Y, and sample Y will correlate better with organ X. But if only these two samples are swapped and there are no external references, then swapping just the two samples' labels would... hmm, actually, if only two samples have their labels swapped, then in the pairwise correlation matrix, samples X and Y would just look like their respective organs are correlated with each other — that is, the correlation between sample X and sample Y would be high (since each carries the other organ's profile, so corr(X,Y) = corr(true Y, true X) — no wait, that's wrong).\n\nWait, let me think carefully. If the labels of organ A and B are swapped: the column labeled \"A\" contains the actual profile of organ B, and the column labeled \"B\" contains the actual profile of organ A. Then the pairwise correlation matrix of the labeled columns: corr(A_labeled, B_labeled) = corr(B_true, A_true) — the same value as before. The correlation matrix is invariant under label swapping! Therefore, whole-genome correlations between samples alone cannot directly detect a two-sample swap — a swap of two labels merely permutes the rows/columns of the correlation matrix.\n\nTherefore, external information is needed: e.g., known organ marker genes and promoter accessibility. The GTF is provided for \"reconstructing gene/promoter accessibility in the same AmexG v6 coordinate system.\" That is, compute promoter accessibility per organ, and check organ-specific marker genes: for example, the column labeled \"Liver\" should show high promoter accessibility for liver marker genes (albumin, etc.), and if it's swapped with say \"Intestine,\" then the liver markers would be highly accessible in the column labeled \"Intestine.\"\n\nThe task states: \"Evidence may combine promoter accessibility, organ marker coherence, and comparison against publicly available source profiles.\"\n\nSo the plan:\n1. Parse the GTF, extract gene TSS positions (protein-coding genes).\n2. Build promoter accessibility: e.g., counts within ±2kb of TSS, aggregated per organ.\n3. Define marker gene sets for axolotl organs (orthologs of known markers) and score each organ column by the coherence of marker accessibility: for each organ pair, check whether the marker genes of organ A are most accessible in the column labeled B, and the markers of B are in the column labeled A.\n4. Alternatively, use cross-species comparison against publicly available source profiles... probably hard to obtain from here; but marker genes can be derived from known biology (mouse/human orthologs).\n\nA simpler and more robust approach without needing external data: use the fact that some organs have very characteristic global signals? But that's weak.\n\nBetter approach: marker coherence. Use known marker genes (axolotl gene names are human-like symbols). Axolotl genome annotation AmexT v47 — gene symbols are probably orthologs of human/mouse symbols.\n\nLet me first look at the GTF to see the gene names.\n\nActually, another approach that doesn't require external markers: for each organ, identify its \"signature\" peaks — the bins where that organ has the highest value across all organs. Then check consistency. But with a swap, the signature of organ A would end up in column B and vice versa. Hmm, that's also circular: the signature bins are computed from the same swapped data.\n\nWait — but there's a clever trick: if the labels of A and B are swapped, then for each of the two samples, the profile of sample A (the true B profile) would be more similar to... hmm, since only two are swapped and everything else is consistent, there's no internal contradiction detectable from the matrix alone. Let me think again: the data matrix has 15 columns. Swap two labels: columns A and B are interchanged. Any statistic computed purely from the matrix that doesn't reference the labels is invariant. Any statistic that references labels (like \"sample Brain should correlate with sample SpinalCord\") requires external knowledge.\n\nTherefore, external knowledge is essential: marker genes. The GTF gives us gene positions. We need prior knowledge about which genes mark which organs. This comes from biological knowledge (e.g., albumin for liver, insulin for pancreas, myosins for heart/limb (muscle), synaptophysin for brain, etc.).\n\nSo the plan:\n1. Load the GTF, extract TSS per gene (gene_id, gene_name, chrom, strand, TSS).\n2. Load the count table in chunks (10kb bins). For each gene, sum counts over promoter windows (TSS ± 2kb or ± 5kb).\n3. Build a promoter accessibility matrix: gene × organ.\n4. Define marker gene sets per organ from literature knowledge (use gene symbols that exist in axolotl annotation).\n5. For each organ pair (i, j), compute the swap score: e.g., score(i,j) = [marker enrichment of organ i's markers in column j + organ j's markers in column i] − [organ i's markers in column i + organ j's markers in column j], normalized by z-score or rank. Also a global coherence check: for each organ, does the set of its marker genes peak in the expected column? Compute a \"coherence\" per organ: the average z-score of its markers in its own column vs. other columns. A swapped organ would show low self-coherence: its markers would be more accessible in another column.\n6. Identify the best pair: for each organ o, find argmax_o' ≠ o of the marker accessibility of organ o in column o'. If there's a mutual best match o→o' and o'→o, that's the swap candidate.\n7. Compute pairwise Pearson correlation of promoter profiles and whole-genome bin profiles to back up the evidence; note that the correlation matrix is label-invariant, but we can show that the promoter profile of sample A correlates better with the marker set of organ B.\n\nAlso \"comparison against publicly available source profiles\" — the task name is ls03-atac-sample-swap; perhaps the original dataset is published. Axolotl ATAC-seq across organs... there's a known paper: \"A chromosome-level genome assembly of the axolotl...\" plus an ATAC atlas? There's an axolotl regeneration atlas with scRNA-seq (e.g., \"Single-cell atlas of axolotl regeneration\"), but I don't recall a bulk ATAC organ atlas. Hmm. In any case, network access may be possible (sandbox says network enabled), but I should be careful about evaluation isolation — use only files under ./inputs. The instructions say \"use only files under ./inputs,\" so external public profiles shouldn't be fetched. I'll rely on marker gene knowledge encoded in the analysis script.\n\nLet me check the GTF structure and gene symbol naming.\n\nThe GTF is 51MB compressed. Parsing with Python's gzip may take a bit, but that's fine.\n\nLet me look at a few lines of the GTF.\n\nAlso, count table: how many rows? Genome ~32Gb / 10kb = ~3.2 million rows. 75MB gzipped, ~3.2M rows × 18 columns — loading with pandas will be a bit heavy but manageable (float32). Actually 3.2M × 15 floats = 48M values = ~190MB in float32. That's fine.\n\nFirst let me check the row count and GTF format."}}
