{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_11","type":"reasoning","text":"The GTF has a gene_name format like \"ZFP37 [nr]|ZNF568 [hs]\" — the gene name includes homology annotations: `[nr]` (non-redundant/axolotl naming) and `[hs]` (human homolog symbol). So I can parse the human ortholog symbol from the `[hs]` tag and map it to known organ marker genes.\n\nPlan:\n1. Parse the GTF's gene lines → gene_id, gene_name (extract human symbol from `[hs]`), chrom, strand, start, end. TSS = start if +, end if -.\n2. Load the count matrix in chunks (10kb bins). For each gene, sum bin counts within the promoter window (e.g., TSS ± 2000 bp — but since bins are 10kb, the ±2kb window usually hits one or two bins; simpler: use bins overlapping the TSS ± 5kb). Actually, with 10kb bins, promoter accessibility is basically the value of the bin containing the TSS, ± adjacent bins. To reduce noise, should I take the max, or the sum of bins overlapping TSS-5kb..TSS+5kb? Hmm, the sum might dilute. General approach: sum counts across bins overlapping the promoter region. For a 10kb resolution, using just the bin containing the TSS is the simplest and still effective. Sum bins within ±5kb of TSS (at most 2 bins), or use the single bin containing the TSS. Let's use bins overlapping [TSS-2000, TSS+2000] (1–2 bins) and sum.\n\nActually a cleaner way: for each gene, identify the bins containing TSS-1kb and TSS+1kb and sum those bins' counts (deduplicated). OK.\n\n3. Marker sets: define human organ marker genes from literature for the 15 organs: Bladder, Brain, Cloaca, GallBladder, Gill, Heart, Intestine, Kidney, Limb, Liver, Lung, Pancreas, Prostate, Spleen, Stomach.\n\nNeed good marker sets. Let me draft (human symbols):\n\n- Brain: SYT1, SNAP25, SYN1, MAP2, TUBB3, NEFL, NEFM, NEFH, GFAP, MBP, PLP1, OLIG1, SOX10, GAD1, GAD2, GRIN1, GRIN2A, GRIN2B, SYP, NRG1, SLITRK?, CHRNA2... Use: SNAP25, SYT1, SYN1, MAP2, TUBB3, NEFL, NEFM, NEFH, GFAP, MBP, SYP, GAD1, GAD2, GRIN1, GRIN2A, GRIN2B, CAMK2A, NMDAR... good.\n- Heart: TNNT2, MYH6, MYH7, MYL2, MYL7, ACTC1, NPPA, NPPB, RYR2, HCN4, SCN5A, TNNI3, GATA4, HAND1, HAND2, TBX5.\n- Liver: ALB, AFP, HNF4A, APOA1, APOB, SERPINA1, CYP3A4, CYP2E1, CYP1A2, AGT, TF (transferrin), FGA, FGB, FGG, APOE, HP, VTG? (axolotl vitellogenin may not be annotated). Use ALB, AFP, HNF4A, APOA1, APOB, SERPINA1, AGT, TF, FGA, FGB, FGG, APOE, CYP3A4, CYP2E1, APOA2, APOC3.\n- Kidney: AQP2, AQP1, SLC12A1, UMOD, SLC12A3, CALB1, NPHS1, NPHS2, PODXL, PAX2, LHX1, GATA3, SLC34A1, SLC34A3, ACE, KNG1? Use AQP2, AQP1, UMOD, SLC12A1, SLC12A3, NPHS1, NPHS2, PODXL, PAX2, LHX1, GATA3, SLC34A1, ACE.\n- Lung: SFTPA1, SFTPB, SFTPC, SFTPD, NKX2-1 (NKX2.1), SCGB1A1, SCGB3A1, MUC5B, AGER, PDPN, HOPX, ABCA3, CHPT1? Use SFTPA1, SFTPB, SFTPC, SFTPD, NKX2-1, SCGB1A1, MUC5B, AGER, PDPN, ABCA3, FOXA2? (FOXA2 also gut).\n- Pancreas: INS, GCG, SST, PPY, PDX1, MAFA, MAFB, NEUROD1, REG1A, CPA1, CPA2, CELA3A, PRSS1, PRSS2, TRY? amylase AMY1A/AMY2A, CEL, CTRB1, CELA2A, PTF1A. Endocrine: INS, GCG, SST, PPY, IAPP. Exocrine: PRSS1, CPA1, CELA3A, AMY1A, CEL, PTF1A, REG1A.\n- Intestine: CDX2, FABP1? (liver-type, actually FABP2 is intestinal), FABP2, VIL1, MUC2, SI (sucrase-isomaltase), APOB? (shared with liver), ALPI, ANPEP (CD13), DPP4, SLC5A1, LCT? (lactase, small intestine), REG4, DEFA5 (defensin, Paneth cells), CHGA? Use CDX2, FABP2, VIL1, MUC2, SI, ALPI, ANPEP, DPP4, SLC5A1, REG4, DEFA5, LCT, MEP1A?\n- Stomach: PGC (pepsinogen C / pepsinogen), PGA3/PGA4/PGA5 (pepsinogens), ATP4A, ATP4B, GAST, SST? (shared), MUC5AC, MUC6, GIF (intrinsic factor), CHIA? LIPF (gastric lipase), TFF1, TFF2, HIST1... Use PGC, PGA3, PGA4, PGA5, ATP4A, ATP4B, GAST, MUC5AC, MUC6, GIF, LIPF, TFF1, TFF2.\n- Spleen: immune markers: CD3E, CD3D, CD3G, CD2, CD7, CD19, MS4A1 (CD20), CD79A, CD79B, LCK, ZAP70, CD8A, CD8B, CD4, GZMB, PRF1, NKG7, GNLY, CD68, CD163, MRC1? Also hemoglobin? Splenic red pulp: HBA, HBB expressed in spleen? Red pulp macrophages. But HBA/HBB is blood, which is also present in all organs. Spleen-specific: hmm. SPI1 (PU.1), LILRB? Use lymphoid markers: CD3E, CD3D, CD2, CD7, LCK, CD19, CD79A, MS4A1, CD79B, PAX5, GZMB, PRF1, NKG7, GNLY, CD8A, CD8B, CD4, IL7R, RAG1?\n- Gill: tricky. Gill markers: ion transporters, hemoglobin (blood in gills), SLC4A1? Gill arch cartilage: COL2A1, SOX9. Respiratory epithelium: mucins? Fish gill markers: NKA ATP1A1, RHCG (Rhesus glycoprotein), SLC26A3/6, carbonic anhydrase CA2/CA4/CA14, V-ATPase. Axolotl gills are larval organ: larval-specific keratins? Might use: CA2? CA4, CA14 (carbonic anhydrases), ATP1A1, RHCG? RHBG, SLC4A1? SLC4A4? Plus hemoglobin HBA/HBB (high blood perfusion in gills). Hmm, but gill has blood → hemoglobin high. Axolotl larval skin/gill keratins. This is the weakest marker set; but gill is unlikely to be swapped.\n- Limb: muscle + connective tissue + skin: MYH1? MYH2? MYH3, MYH4? Limb is mostly muscle/cartilage/skin: MYH7? heart-specific; skeletal: MYH1, MYH2, MYH4, ACTA1, MYOG? MYOD1, MYF5? Structural: COL1A1, COL1A2 (ubiquitous), TNC, COMP (cartilage), ACAN, COL2A1 (cartilage), COL10A1, SOX9, KRT14? KRT5? (skin/epidermis), DMBT1? Axolotl limb blastema... Use: ACTA1, MYH1, MYH2, MYH3, MYH4, MYOD1, MYOG, TNNC2, TNNT3? (fast skeletal muscle TNNC2/TNNT3), CKM (muscle creatine kinase), PYGM (muscle glycogen phosphorylase), ACAN, COMP, COL2A1, SOX9, KRT14, KRT5.\n- Bladder: urothelium: UPII (UPK2), UPK1A, UPK1B, UPK3A, UPK3B, KRT20 (umbrella cells), KRT7, KRT5 (basal), PPARG? GATA3? Also smooth muscle: ACTA2, MYH11 (also in many organs). Use UPK1A, UPK1B, UPK2, UPK3A, UPK3B, KRT20, KRT7, KRT5, FOXA2? No. PPARg. Also bladder smooth muscle.\n- GallBladder: SLC26A6? CFTR? Gallbladder epithelial markers: SLC4A2? MUC1? Hmm. Gallbladder cholangiocyte markers: SLC4A2, SLC26A6, CFTR, BEST2? MUC5AC? AQP1? Cholangiocyte markers: KRT19, KRT7 (also shared with bladder), SOX9, HNF1B, HNF4A (shared with liver), SPP1, MUC1. Use KRT19, KRT7, HNF1B, SOX9, SLC26A6, CFTR, MUC1, AQP1, SPP1.\n- Cloaca: hindgut/urogenital junction: caudal-type Hox genes: HOXA13, HOXD13, HOXC... KRTs? Markers unclear. Cloacal epithelium. Maybe treat like a mix of hindgut/intestine + urothelium: KRT7? Use caudal Hox: HOXA13, HOXD13, HOXC13? Plus maybe KRT20? Weak but that's OK.\n- Prostate: KLK3 (PSA), KLK2, KLK4, ACPP (PAP), NKX3-1, HOXB13, FOXA1? AR (androgen receptor), TMPRSS2, MSMB, AZGP1, STEAP4? Use KLK3, KLK2, KLK4, ACPP, NKX3-1, HOXB13, AR, TMPRSS2, MSMB.\n\nNote: Do female axolotls have a prostate? Well, whatever, the data has a Prostate column.\n\n4. Scoring:\n   - For each organ o, for each column c, compute the marker score: mean z-score of organ o's marker genes' promoter accessibility across genes within column c (z-score across columns per gene). That is, for each gene g, compute its accessibility profile across 15 organs, standardize per gene (z = (x - mean)/std across organs), then the marker set score for (o, c) = mean over g in markers(o) of z[g, c].\n   - Coherence: for each organ o, self-score s(o,o) and best alternative s(o, c*). If the labels are consistent, s(o,o) should be the maximum. If organ o is swapped with organ p, then s(o, p) > s(o, o), and s(o,p) is maximum, and vice versa.\n   - Pairwise swap score: swap_score(o,p) = [s(o,p) - s(o,o)] + [s(p,o) - s(p,p)], perhaps normalized. Larger = stronger evidence of a swap between labels o and p.\n   - Rank pairs by swap_score; rank=1 is the top.\n   - Uniqueness: the top score should be clearly positive and well-separated from the others (e.g., margin vs. the 2nd, or vs. the max score across all other pairs). Also check mutual best: p = argmax_c s(o,c), o = argmax_c s(p,c).\n\n5. Evidence for swap_call.json: promoter accessibility of marker genes (specific genes), correlation numbers, and also a genome-wide correlation check: after correcting the swap, does each organ correlate better with the expected marker profile?\n\nAlso, REFERENCE_NOTES asks sample_similarity.csv to have rows for all unordered organ pairs with columns organ_a, organ_b, swap_score, rank, evidence_type. 15 organs → C(15,2)=105 rows.\n\nAlso, as an orthogonal signal, let me think about chromosome-level/autosome coverage... probably unnecessary.\n\nAnother orthogonal signal that doesn't depend on marker knowledge: X-chromosome dosage compensation / sex chromosomes? Axolotl sex chromosomes: ZW system, female heterogametic. If two organs were from individuals of different sexes, coverage of the sex chromosomes would differ, but... that doesn't apply to a label swap.\n\nAlternative internal check: for a pair of samples, if their label swap is swapped, then the pairwise correlation of those two samples vs. other organs... as mentioned above, label-invariant. So markers are the key.\n\nPotential issue: gene symbols in the GTF are in a format like \"ZFP37 [nr]|ZNF568 [hs]\". Some genes may only have \"[nr]\" names (LOC…). Parse the `[hs]` symbol. Also multiple homologs can be listed. Extract the human symbol token before \" [hs]\".\n\nNote: gene_name in the GTF: \"ZFP37 [nr]|ZNF568 [hs]\" — so segments are separated by \"|\", each segment is \"SYMBOL [tag]\". Collect segments tagged with [hs].\n\nNow, about the data: 10kb bins across the whole genome, probably all bins (including zero bins). 3.2M rows. Load with pandas in chunks. For each gene, we need bins overlapping the promoter. Efficient approach: build a dictionary keyed by (chrom, bin_start) for fast lookup? A 3.2M-row dict keyed by (chrom, bin_start) is memory-hungry but OK (~3.2M entries). Alternative: for each gene, compute the bin index containing the TSS: start_bin = TSS//10000*10000. Then look up in a per-chromosome array indexed by bin index: store counts as a dict of chrom → numpy array indexed by bin index. Genome size ~32e9 / 10kb = 3.2e6 values × 15 organs × float32 = 192MB. Manageable.\n\nBut wait — the genome size per chrom.sizes: e.g., chr1p_1 = 472,565,920 → 47256 bins. Total ~ sum(sizes)/10000. Sum of the listed sizes: roughly 60 chromosomes × ~450Mb ≈ 30.5Gb → ~3.05M bins. OK.\n\nPlan: read the TSV with pandas.read_csv(sep='\\t', chunksize=500000). For each chunk, for each row map (chrom, start, end) → value; but more simply: precompute per-chromosome arrays sized ceil(chrom_size/10000)*... Actually bins start at 10000 (the first bin in the file: start=10000, end=20000). Interesting — bin indices are start=10000. So bin_start//10000 gives an index of 1 or greater. Allocate arrays sized max_start//10000+2.\n\nMemory: 3.05M bins × 15 columns float32 = 183MB. OK.\n\nThen for each gene: TSS position (1-based). Promoter window: TSS-2000..TSS+2000 → bins overlapping that window: floor((TSS-2001)/10000)+... hmm. Bin [start, end), where start is a multiple of 10000 and start≥10000? The first bin is 10000-20000. What about the region 0-10000? Maybe absent. Handle missing bins with 0/ignore.\n\nFor gene TSS t (1-based): window [t-2000, t+2000]. Overlapping bins: bin_start ranges from floor((t-2000)/10000)*10000 to floor((t+2000)/10000)*10000, with bin_start ≥ 10000? Hmm wait, actually the bins appear to start at 10000. Maybe there's also a bin 0-10000; I've only looked at the first few rows. Let me check the first rows of each chromosome later; simplest: build arrays with offset: index = bin_start//10000.\n\nPromoter accessibility per gene-organ = sum of counts in overlapping bins. Use a ±2kb window (covers 1–2 bins).\n\nThen compute the z-score matrix (genes × organs) and marker scores.\n\nAlso compute pairwise Pearson correlations of genome-wide profiles between organs (label-invariant, but for context/reporting), and maybe also compare promoter profiles of organ pairs to marker sets.\n\nAlso, as a sanity check for marker sets: verify that each organ's markers are enriched in its own column among organs not involved in the swap.\n\nEdge case considerations: the swapped organs might be ones with similar biology, e.g., GallBladder↔Liver, Stomach↔Intestine, Bladder↔Cloaca, Gill↔Lung? Hmm, the task is \"determine whether