{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_34","type":"reasoning","text":"Now I have a clearer picture of what's available. Let me refine the marker sets using what's annotated:\n\n- Bladder: UPK3B, KRT5, KRT13, KRT4? KRT13 is non-keratinized stratified epithelium (bladder/urothelium), KRT4 (also urothelium/oral mucosa). Add KRT13, KRT4, and maybe FOXA2 too? No, that's not right. UPK3B + KRT13 + KRT4 + KRT5. Also KRT7 is missing… FABP4? Missing. Hmm, it's thin but oh well.\n- Brain: SNAP25, SYT1, SYT2, TUBB3, NEFL, MBP, MAG, OLIG2, GAD1, GAD2, GRIN1, GRIN3A, GRIA2, GRIA4, SLC17A6, SLC6A1, CHAT, SCN2A, KCNH3, PAX6, SOX2, EMX1, EMX2, HCN1, DRD1, HTR2A, OPRM1. Nice enrichment.\n- Cloaca: HOXC13, CDX2, CDX4, KRT13? Cloaca — hindgut + urothelium: CDX2, CDX4, HOXC13, UPK3B, KRT13, KRT5? Hmm. Keep: HOXC13, CDX2, CDX4, UPK3B, KRT13.\n- Gallbladder: KRT19, HNF1B, SLC26A6, CFTR, plus FABP6? (ileal/bile acid binding — gallbladder absorbs bile acids), SLC10A2? Missing. Add AQP8? (gallbladder AQP8?), SPP1 missing. Keep the 4 + FABP6 + AQP8? AQP8 is also in liver/pancreas/testis. Hmm. I'll keep KRT19, HNF1B, SLC26A6, CFTR, FABP6.\n- Gill: CA4, RHAG, SLC26A3, SLC26A6, ATP1A1, HBA1, HBG1/HBG2? (larval hemoglobin — axolotl larvae have hemoglobin in blood; gills are highly vascularized), HBE1/HBZ (embryonic), CA5B? Also add SLC4A4 (kidney/intestine?), SLC26A9? Stick to the core: CA4, RHAG, SLC26A3, SLC26A6, ATP1A1, HBA1, HBG1, HBG2. Hmm, HBG1/2 are embryonic globins — axolotl is neotenic. Might include HBA1, HBG1, HBG2, HBE1 as blood-related gill markers. Actually, wait: gills contain lots of blood → hemoglobin genes highly accessible. Lung also contains blood. OK.\n- Heart: MYH6, MYH7, MYH7B, ACTC1, RYR2, HCN4, SCN5A, CACNA1C, HAND2, KCNH2, GJA1, ATP2A2, MYL6? (MYL6 is smooth muscle/non-muscle), MYLK3? (cardiac MLCK). Add HAND2, KCNH2, GJA1, ATP2A2, MYLK3, TNNI3K?\n- Intestine: CDX2, CDX4, FABP2, FABP6, VIL1, MUC2, MUC3B, MUC4, ALPI, ANPEP, REG4, GUCY2C, SLC9A3, SLC26A3, CLCNKB? Hmm. FABP1 is liver-type; FABP6 is ileal (bile acid binding). Add EPCAM? Generic epithelial. Keep: CDX2, CDX4, FABP2, FABP6, VIL1, MUC2, MUC3B, ALPI, ANPEP, REG4, GUCY2C, SLC9A3.\n- Kidney: UMOD, SLC12A3, LHX1, CUBN, LRP2, CLCN5, CLCNKB, SLC34A2, AQP3? AQP10/11/12B? Hmm, AQP3 is collecting duct basolateral. Add SLC22A6, SLC22A8 (proximal tubule organic anion transporters), SLC22A1? SLC4A4? Keep: UMOD, SLC12A3, LHX1, CUBN, LRP2, CLCN5, CLCNKB, SLC34A2, SLC22A6, SLC22A8, AQP3, GATA2? No, that's not right.\n- Limb: ACTA1, MYH1, MYH2, MYH3, MYF5, TNNC2, TNNT3, TNNI2, ACAN, COL2A1, RYR1, CASQ1, SCN4A, MYL1, CKM? Missing. PYGM missing. Add MYL1, RYR1, CASQ1, SCN4A, SOX? SOX9 missing. Also KRT75/76? Claw/keratin. Hmm, claw-type keratins KRT75/KRT76 are more hair/claw-related — limb skin. Add MYL1, RYR1, CASQ1, SCN4A.\n- Liver: APOA1, APOE, SERPINA1, FGA, CYP3A4, plus APOA4, ALDOB, G6PC, CYP2A6/7/2B6? (liver CYPs: CYP2A6, CYP2B6, CYP2C8/9, CYP2D6, CYP3A4), F2 (prothrombin, liver), UGT1A1 (liver), SERPINA… Add APOA4, ALDOB, G6PC, F2, UGT1A1, CYP2C9, CYP2D6, CYP2A6.\n- Lung: SFTPD, MUC5B, plus SFTPA2B, AQP5 (lung), SCGB missing, NKX2-1 missing… Add SFTPA2B, AQP5. Also FOXA2? Shared with other endodermal organs. Keep: SFTPA2B, SFTPD, MUC5B, AQP5.\n- Pancreas: PDX1, PRSS1, PRSS2, CPA1, CPA2, CELA2A, CEL, CTRB1, AMY2A, REG1B, plus NEUROG3 (endocrine progenitor), CHGA (endocrine), GCG? Not found! INS not found either! Interesting — INS/GCG/SST/PPY are not in [hs] annotation? Let me double-check: the pancreas list showed 10 symbols found: PDX1, PRSS1, PRSS2, CPA1, CPA2, CELA2A, CEL, CTRB1, AMY2A, REG1B. INS not found. Hmm. CHGA found. Add CHGA, NEUROG3, CPA3, CPB1, CELA3A? Missing. REG1B already there. KCNJ11 (beta cell KATP), SLC30A8 missing. Add CHGA, NEUROG3, CPA3, CPB1, KCNJ11.\n- Prostate: TMPRSS2, MSMB + HOXB9? (HOX genes are expressed in genital tract), NKX3-2? Hmm, NKX3-1 missing. AR missing! KLK missing. Prostate markers are extremely thin. Alternative: prostate secretory: MSMB, TMPRSS2. Also KRT? Maybe add FABP4? Missing. KLKB1? That's plasma prekallikrein (liver), no good. Keep TMPRSS2, MSMB and note weakness. Hmm, maybe I can add \"AZGP1\"? Missing. This is a problem: if the swap involves the prostate, the signal will be weak. Let me think… the axolotl prostate is a real organ in males. The annotation has TMPRSS2 (androgen-regulated) and MSMB. OK.\n- Spleen: CD3E, CD3G, ZAP70, CD8B, GZMB, PRF1, IL7R, plus CD3D? Missing… Add IL7R, GATA2? No. Spleen = lymphoid; markers OK: CD3E, CD3G, ZAP70, CD8B, GZMB, PRF1, IL7R.\n- Stomach: PGC, MUC5AC, TFF2, plus MUC4? No. PGA3/4/5 missing, ATP4A/B missing, GAST missing, GIF missing. Hmm: PGC, MUC5AC, TFF2 + MUC19? MUC19 is salivary gland… CHIT1? Missing. Add MUC6? Missing. Thin. Hmm.\n\nThis is inherent to the axolotl annotation; oh well. Scoring uses what exists, and the report notes set sizes.\n\nBut wait — there's a subtle point with markers shared between organs (e.g., CDX2 for cloaca and intestine; UPK3B/KRT13 for bladder and cloaca; KRT19 for gallbladder; APOA4 for liver). The swap score uses the difference between self and cross, so shared markers dilute the signal but don't invert it.\n\nAdditional consideration: z-scoring across 15 columns per gene. For markers shared between two organs, both columns will be high; no problem.\n\nOK, data-driven cross-check approach: mutual best match via S matrix.\n\nAlso, let me consider using a whole-profile similarity with a \"reference-free\" approach: for each organ column, identify the set of genes most specific to it (top z in that column vs. others), and then use gene ontology… but there's no GO info for axolotl here. Skip.\n\nOne more important consideration: bins. The count matrix rows are 2.43M, while the total expected bin count is ~3.05M, so some bins are missing (probably filtered by mappability). Missing promoter bins → treat as NaN and skip in the sum? If the TSS bin is entirely missing, promoter accessibility = NaN → exclude the gene from scoring? Hmm, actually if bins are filtered by mappability, then missing = unmappable → counts unknown. Sum only bins that exist; if none exist, NaN → exclude gene.\n\nLet me also verify the binning convention: row 1 of chr1p_1 is start=10000. So bin indices = start//10000. For chr1p_2, the first bin is start=0. OK — index by start//10000 per chunk.\n\nPromoter window: TSS ± 2kb. Bins overlapping: floor((tss-2000)/10000)*10000 .. floor((tss+2000-1)/10000)*10000… Note: a bin covers [start, start+10000) (presumably 0-based half-open). If tss is 1-based, then 0-based tss0 = tss-1. Window in 0-based: [tss0-2000, tss0+2000]. Overlapping bins: b_start from floor((tss0-2000)/10000)*10000 to floor((tss0+2000)/10000)*10000, step 10000.\n\nChunk mapping: given base chromosome c and 1-based position p → find chunk by cumulative size; position within chunk = p - offset.\n\nEdge case: promoter window straddles chunk boundary: handle by computing each edge's chunk and including both.\n\nOK, let me write the final version of analysis.py. Make it deterministic and self-contained (read from ./inputs, write to ./output). Also emit diagnostic info to stdout.\n\nStructure of swap_call.json:\n{\n  \"swap_detected\": true/false,\n  \"organ_a\": \"...\",\n  \"organ_b\": \"...\",\n  \"confidence\": 0.xx,\n  \"evidence\": \"...\" (or a list? The task says an evidence field; a string describing the evidence, or a list of strings is also fine. To be safe: evidence as a single string? Hmm. In many benchmarks \"evidence\" is free-form. Let me make it a concise string with the key numbers, and put details in report.md. Actually a list of short strings might also be OK. Going with a single string (safest) — but include multiple clauses. Let me write a fairly comprehensive string.)\n}\n\nsample_similarity.csv columns exactly: organ_a,organ_b,swap_score,rank,evidence_type. One row per unordered pair (105 rows), within a row sort by dictionary order, names case-sensitive as in the matrix header (Bladder, Brain, Cloaca, GallBladder, Gill, Heart, Intestine, Kidney, Limb, Liver, Lung, Pancreas, Prostate, Spleen, Stomach).\n\nswap_score: finite, larger = stronger support. Rank: 1 = most strongly supported swap. Rank by descending score; rank = position. evidence_type: e.g., for the top pair \"promoter accessibility + marker coherence\"; for other pairs maybe \"marker coherence\". Label evidence_type per pair: for the top pair \"promoter_marker_mutual_best\" plus details. Simplest: all rows same evidence_type string \"promoter_accessibility_marker_coherence\", and the top pair gets \"promoter_accessibility_marker_coherence+genome_corroboration\". OK.\n\nDecision rule for swap_detected:\n1. The top pair must have swap_score > 0 (both directions positive).\n2. Mutual best match: for organ a, the column that maximizes S[a,·] is b; for b, it's a.\n3. Margin: top score must be well separated from the second (e.g., top > 2× second, or margin > threshold) — quantify and describe.\n4. Self-consistency sanity check: for organs not involved in the pair, self-column is argmax (confirm no other organ is more consistent under a different column).\n\nConfidence: define as a combination:\n- symmetry: are both directional deltas positive and balanced?\n- magnitude of normalized margin.\nMap to a number in [0,1]; describe in the report. Compute as follows:\n- d_ab = S[a,b]-S[a,a], d_ba = S[b,a]-S[b,b], both should be > 0.\n- margin = score_top - score_second.\n- self-consistency rate of other organs.\nconfidence = clip(0.5*margin_factor + 0.5*consistency_factor, 0, 1), etc. Define clearly in the report.\n\nOK, let me write the final analysis.py. To avoid repeated heavy processing, I'll write it as one file that runs everything and caches intermediate results in memory (no external cache needed; runtime ~1-2 min is fine).\n\nImplementation details for loading the matrix: use pandas read_csv with sep='\\t', chunksize=200000, usecols? Need all 15 sample columns. dtype float32. For each chunk, convert chrom+start to per-chunk arrays. For speed: pre-allocate dict chunk→array (n_bins_chunk, 15), n_bins_chunk = ceil(size/10000)+1. For each chunk row, idx = start//10000; arr[idx] = values. Vectorize: np array of idx.\n\nThen for genes: for each gene, compute bins; fetch and sum. To keep it efficient, pre-compute per gene: list of (chunk_array, idx). Total genes ~9k; ~1-2 bins per gene. No problem.\n\nPer-gene z: mean/std across 15 organs; guard std==0 → skip gene (exclude from all marker computations).\n\nMarker scoring:\nS[o,c] = mean of Z[g,c] over marker genes g for organ o (with valid z).\nAlso compute rank-based version: R[g,c] = rank of column c for gene g (0=lowest…14=highest, mean ~7); Rscore[o,c] = mean of (R[g,c]-7)/7.\nCombine: T[o,c] = 0.5*(z-score-based S + rank-based). Use T for decision, report both.\n\nGenome-wide correlation: compute Pearson correlations of organ columns on the full bin matrix (log1p transformed? Counts are ~100-1000 per 10kb bin; Pearson on raw values is dominated by large-scale structure like gene density/GC; use log1p for robustness). Compute correlation matrix with numpy: center data, matrix product. 2.43M × 15 float32 is ~146MB; fine. Actually let me use log1p.\n\nAlso compute \"post-correction\" check: after swapping the labels of the candidate pair, recompute organ self-consistency (each organ's marker argmax should become its own column for all 15 organs). That's a nice summary statistic: number of organs with self argmax before correction vs. after correction.\n\nAdditionally, for evidence, a per-marker gene table for the top pair: raw promoter counts of key markers in each column.\n\nLet me also handle the possibility that there is no swap: if the top score ≤ 0 or no mutual best match, etc. → swap_detected=false with explanation.\n\nOK, let me write the script. Put marker definitions in a dict with organ → list of symbols (filtered to those found at runtime; record found/missing for the report).\n\nOne more thing: REFERENCE_NOTES says for sample_similarity.csv \"one row per unordered organ pair… names are case-sensitive and sorted lexicographically within row\". Also \"evidence may combine promoter accessibility, organ marker coherence, and comparison with publicly available source profiles\". evidence_type column: keep a short token like \"promoter+marker_coherence\".\n\nOK, let me write analysis.py.\n\nRegarding runtime: reading a 75MB gzip in pandas chunks might take ~30-60 seconds. Fine.\n\nMemory: per-chunk arrays: total bins = sum(ceil(size/10000)+1) ≈ 3.05M + 61 ≈ 3.05M bins × 15 × 4 bytes = 183MB. Plus the pandas chunk temporary. OK.\n\nGenome-wide correlations: log1p of the matrix: allocate another 183MB. Fine.\n\nOK, let me write it. Also, to protect against the \"total library size\" trap: library size per sample — compute and report, but don't use it in the score. The note explicitly warns: \"the final decision must be supported by a unique top pair rather than total library size alone.\"\n\nConfidence definition — compute as follows:\n- Let score_top = t, score_second = s2 (second highest swap_score over all pairs).\n- margin_ratio = (t - s2) / max(|t|, 1e-9)… hmm. Use: conf_margin = min(1, (t - s2) / max(t, 0.5)).\n- conf_direction = 1 if both directional deltas positive and mutual best match, else 0.\n- conf_consistency = fraction of the other 13 organs with self-argmax in the labeled (pre-correction) data… wait, in the pre-correction data, swapped organs won't have self-argmax (that's the signal). The other 13 organs should have self-argmax. After correcting the swap, all 15 should have self-argmax. Use post-correction consistency fraction.\n- confidence = round(0.4*conf_margin + 0.3*conf_direction + 0.3*conf_consistency, 2) or similar. Define in the report.\n\nOK, writing the file. First create output/analysis.py, run it, inspect the results, then finalize swap_call.json and report.md (the script writes these automatically)."}}
