{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_40","type":"reasoning","text":"After CPM normalization, the picture has changed but is still noisy:\n\n- Brain markers: Brain column 0.86 is the max ✓ (self-consistent)\n- Pancreas markers: Pancreas 1.16 is max ✓\n- Lung markers: Lung 0.94 is max ✓\n- Heart markers: Heart 0.70 is max ✓\n- Liver markers: Liver 0.70 is max ✓\n- Bladder markers: max is Gill 1.33, self is 0.09 ✗ → the profile of Bladder markers lives in the Gill column?!\n- Kidney markers: max is Gill 0.88, self is 0.19 ✗\n- Cloaca markers: max is Stomach 0.79, self is 0.24\n- Stomach markers: max is Heart 0.74, self is -0.30?! Hmm, this is odd — stomach markers highest in the Heart column?\n- Gill markers: max is Liver 0.47? self is -0.05. Weird.\n- Limb markers: max is Gill 0.64, self 0.36.\n- Intestine markers: max is Stomach 0.60, self 0.27.\n- GallBladder markers: max is Intestine 0.52? self 0.21.\n- Prostate markers: max is Heart 0.51? self 0.41 (close).\n- Spleen markers: max is Heart 0.28, self -0.03.\n\nThis looks messy. Some of this is noise from small marker sets (Cloaca 7 marker genes, Prostate 4 genes, GallBladder 10). But there are consistent signals: many marker sets peak in the \"Gill\" column (Bladder 1.33, Kidney 0.88, Cloaca 0.74, Limb 0.64, Spleen 0.23, GallBladder 0.16…). Hmm, many sets are high in Gill. And \"Stomach markers\" peak in Heart (0.74), \"Intestine markers\" peak in Stomach (0.60), \"Liver markers\" also slightly high in Stomach (0.32)…\n\nWait. Maybe the data actually has multiple anomalies, or my marker sets are poor / not well-matched to axolotl biology, or the z-scoring approach is still confounded by something else (e.g., GC content / mappability differences between samples, or batch effects).\n\nLet me think about the Gill column: the Gill column has high accessibility for many marker sets. What if the Gill column's profile is actually a mixture? Or maybe gill tissue in bulk ATAC simply has very broad accessibility (gills are a respiratory/immune/osmoregulatory organ with many cell types)? Hmm.\n\nAnother possibility: the z-score approach is dominated by a small number of high-variance genes. Let me try a cleaner method: instead of per-gene z-scores across organs, compute a per-gene \"specificity\" weight and score organs by the mean rank. Or use a correlation-based template approach: build the expected organ profile from markers, and compute each column's correlation with each marker template… but that's essentially the same thing.\n\nA deeper question: is the swap even detectable with my marker knowledge? The true swap might involve organs whose markers I can capture well: e.g., Stomach↔Intestine? Note that Intestine markers peak in the Stomach column (0.60 vs self 0.27) — and Stomach markers peak… in Heart (0.74)? That breaks the mutual-best-match story for Stomach↔Intestine. Hmm, but wait: Stomach markers in the Intestine column = 0.32, in the Heart column = 0.74. If Stomach↔Intestine is the swap, then Stomach markers should be highest in the Intestine column. 0.74 (Heart) > 0.32 (Intestine). Doesn't fit cleanly.\n\nLet me look at the actual top marker genes per organ and see which columns they prefer — with the raw CPM values. The mean can be dominated by one or two genes. Let me examine the details per organ. Also, maybe I should restrict to the most organ-specific markers (the ones whose known biology is most clearly defined).\n\nAlso, the Heart column looks like an \"attractor\": Stomach markers 0.74 in Heart, Prostate markers 0.51 in Heart, Spleen 0.28 in Heart. Heart is a muscle organ, and its chromatin is dominated by muscle genes; the Stomach marker set includes… hmm, PGC, MUC5AC, TFF2, MUC4. MUC4 is a generic mucin. Hmm wait — I included MUC4 in the stomach set (MUC4 is expressed in gastric surface mucous cells, but also in many other epithelia). Let me check what's driving it.\n\nAnother possibility: is there something wrong with the Heart column, like contamination? Or is the Heart sample actually the Stomach sample?? If Heart↔Stomach is the swap: the Heart column should contain the stomach profile → stomach markers high in the Heart column (0.74 ✓), and heart markers high in the Stomach column. Heart markers in the Stomach column = 0.05 ✗ (max is Heart 0.70). Doesn't fit.\n\nHmm. Let me check the top pairs more carefully and investigate per-gene details. Let me dump, for each marker set, the per-gene argmax column and the CPM values. Also compute the fraction of marker genes whose argmax column is each organ.\n\nAlso, maybe I should rethink the normalization: CPM over all bins includes heterochromatic bins; in ATAC, TMM or median-ratio normalization might be better. But CPM should be fine.\n\nAnother possibility: batch/sex effects. Let me check the global correlation structure between columns (after log1p+CPM) to see which columns cluster together. That's label-invariant, but it can reveal, e.g., whether the Gill column correlates with the Kidney column (suggesting Gill contains kidney tissue or vice versa), etc. For a two-label swap, columns a and b still correlate with each other just as they did before the swap; clustering shows biological similarity between the true profiles: e.g., if the true profiles of Gill and Kidney are similar, they cluster together. That in itself doesn't identify the swap, but combined with marker analysis it helps: e.g., if the Bladder markers peak in the Gill column, and Gill's profile clusters with the Bladder column's profile… that's a clue: the Gill column contains a Bladder-like profile → check whether the Bladder column contains a Gill-like profile (Gill markers in the Bladder column = -0.20, low; Gill markers max in Liver 0.47). Hmm.\n\nHmm, Gill markers: CA4, CA5B, RHAG, SLC26A3, SLC26A6, SLC26A9, ATP1A1, HBA1, HBG1, HBG2. Liver column 0.47… CA genes? Carbonic anhydrase is also expressed in the liver. HBG1/2 embryonic globins might be high in the liver (the larval liver is a hematopoietic organ!). So the Gill marker set is contaminated by liver hematopoiesis. My Gill set is weak.\n\nGiven this mess, let me step back and think about the organs with strong, clean signals:\n- Brain markers → Brain ✓\n- Pancreas markers → Pancreas ✓\n- Lung markers → Lung ✓\n- Heart markers → Heart ✓\n- Liver markers → Liver ✓\n- Kidney markers → Gill (0.88) > Kidney (0.19)\n- Bladder markers → Gill (1.33) > Bladder (0.09)\n- Limb markers → Gill (0.64) > Limb (0.36)\n- Spleen markers → Heart (0.28) > Spleen (-0.03)\n- Intestine markers → Stomach (0.60) > Intestine (0.27)\n- Stomach markers → Heart (0.74) > GallBladder (0.06)… self -0.30\n- Cloaca markers → Stomach (0.79) > Intestine (0.53) > self (0.24)\n- GallBladder markers → Intestine (0.52) > Stomach (0.30) > self (0.21)\n- Prostate markers → Heart (0.51) > Prostate (0.41) > Stomach (0.32)\n\nMany \"max\" values are modest (0.2–0.6) and driven by few genes. The standout anomalies are: Bladder markers → Gill (1.33, strong) and Kidney markers → Gill (0.88, strong). Both uro-renal! Bladder and kidney are both part of the renal/urinary system. Gill has high accessibility at both kidney and bladder marker promoters.\n\nHmm, interesting: what if the Gill column is actually a mixture (e.g., a doublet sample)? Or what if the true identity of the Gill sample is… kidney? But then kidney markers should be high in Gill ✓ (0.88), and gill markers should be high in the Kidney column: Gill markers in Kidney column = 0.06 ✗. Not mutual.\n\nWhat if the true identity of the Gill column were bladder? Bladder markers in Gill = 1.33 ✓✓; then gill markers should be high in the Bladder column: -0.20 ✗.\n\nHmm, neither is mutual.\n\nLet me look at the per-gene details to understand what's driving these. Dump the CPM values of the marker genes for the Bladder, Kidney, Gill, Stomach, Intestine, and Heart sets.\n\nAlso — maybe the promoter window is too narrow / noisy at 10kb resolution. Let me widen to ±5kb, or take the max of overlapping bin