{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_45","type":"reasoning","text":"Very informative. Observations:\n\n1. Many \"organ-specific\" marker genes in the axolotl annotation are clearly not behaving specifically: multiple KRT19, KRT5, MUC5AC, MUC4, SCN2A, PRSS2, CELA2A genes have weird profiles. The axolotl genome is full of lineage-specific duplications, and the human [hs] orthology assignment is often wrong/misleading (e.g., \"SCN2A\" genes that are actually something else; multiple \"MUC5AC\" copies with ubiquitous profiles). This is injecting a huge amount of noise.\n\n2. But there are also clean ones: Pancreas exocrine genes (CPA1/2, CPB1, CTRB1, PRSS1, AMY2A, CELA2A copies) → strongly Pancreas-specific ✓. Lung: SFTPA2B, SFTPD, MUC5B (one copy), AQP5 → Lung ✓. Brain subset: TUBB3, SLC6A1, SOX2, GRIN1, NEFL, SYT1 → Brain ✓. Heart markers also look clean (from the truncated output). Liver markers: APOA1 etc. probably also clean.\n\n3. Gill problem: Bladder marker UPK3B (one copy) → Gill 2.4 > Bladder 0.9; KRT4 → Gill 2.1 > Bladder 0.5. Kidney markers: which genes are driving Gill's high values? The truncated output was hiding the Kidney section. Let me think about which kidney genes are pointing to Gill: probably SLC12A3? UMOD? Need to check.\n\nLet me look at the middle section of the truncated output (Heart, Intestine, Kidney, Limb, Liver). Need those. But first, think about the \"Gill\" pattern: Bladder markers (UPK3B, KRT4) are high in the Gill column; kidney markers also high in Gill. If the true swap is Bladder↔Gill, then the Gill column would have the bladder profile (✓ for UPK3B/KRT4), and the Bladder column should have the gill profile. Gill markers in the Bladder column: low (-0.20). But my gill marker set is weak (CA5B self 2.8 ✓ actually good, HBG globins are high in lung/spleen — hematopoietic). Hmm.\n\nWait, actually, let me reconsider: maybe the true identity of the Gill column is KIDNEY? Kidney markers are high in Gill (0.88 overall). Then the Kidney column should have the gill profile: Gill markers in Kidney column = 0.06, weak.\n\nHmm. What about this: the Gill column is high in both kidney markers and bladder markers — uro-renal lineage. In axolotl, the kidney (mesonephros) and bladder/urodeum… Gill is ectodermal/endodermal. Kidney+bladder markers being high in gill is odd.\n\nWait — maybe I'm thinking about this backwards. The \"Gill\" column has the kidney/bladder profile because the sample labeled \"Gill\" is actually… no wait, if the gill sample were actually bladder, then the bladder markers should peak in the Gill column ✓ (1.33!). Then where's the gill profile? It should be in the Bladder column. Gill markers in Bladder column: -0.20 — no good.\n\nUnless… the true gill profile doesn't resemble my \"gill marker set\" at all (weak set: CA5B is good, but CA5B in Gill column is 2.8 self ✓!). Wait, CA5B's self=2.8 means CA5B is indeed highest in the Gill column. So the Gill column has some genuine gill signal (at least CA5B). Hmm, but if the Gill column were actually bladder, CA5B shouldn't peak in Gill.\n\nHold on, let me reconsider: maybe it's not Gill that's swapped — maybe it's BLADDER that's swapped. Bladder's own markers are low in the Bladder column (0.09). The bladder profile is in… the Gill column (1.33). If Bladder↔X is the swap, then the Bladder column contains X's profile, and the X column contains the bladder profile. The bladder profile peaks in Gill → X=Gill. Then the Bladder column should contain the gill profile. Gill markers in Bladder column = -0.20. Fail.\n\nHmm, wait, but maybe my gill marker set is just wrong. If the gill marker set is wrong (genes not actually gill-specific), then even if Bladder↔Gill were truly swapped, \"gill markers in Bladder column\" would be low. The strong direction is: bladder markers in Gill column = 1.33 (driven by UPK3B 2.4 vs self 0.9, KRT4 2.1 vs 0.5, KRT5 copies).\n\nAlso, kidney markers in Gill column = 0.88 — which kidney genes? Need to see. If UMOD/SLC12A3 (very kidney-specific) peak in Gill, that's strong evidence that Gill column = kidney-like…\n\nHmm, wait a moment. What if the swap isn't involving Gill at all, and instead the GILL sample is a genuine gill that just happens to have high accessibility at kidney/bladder promoters? Seems unlikely.\n\nLet me think about the Stomach↔Heart anomaly: stomach markers (mostly MUC5AC/MUC4/PGC/TFF2) peak in Heart?? Looking at the details: MUC5AC copies are high in Heart, Prostate, Spleen, Kidney — essentially noise, not stomach-specific in this annotation. PGC peaks in Liver/Cloaca/Stomach (4.7/5.5/5.5) — nearly stomach-specific ✓ (PGC self 4.7, Cloaca 5.5, Liver 5.5). Hmm, PGC (pepsinogen C) also in Cloaca/Liver? Odd. TFF2 → Bladder/Gill/Heart 1.3 vs self 0.7. MUC4 copies → GallBladder/Intestine/Stomach — somewhat intestinal.\n\nSo the Stomach set is mostly garbage (mucin paralogs with non-specific profiles), except PGC.\n\nIntestine markers in Stomach column 0.60 vs self 0.27: driven by CDX2 (Stomach 4.0, Intestine 3.5!), CDX4 (Stomach 3.7 vs Intestine 2.4), FABP6 (Cloaca 8.6! Stomach 7.3, Intestine 7.2), RHAG?? (Intestine section truncated). Interesting: CDX2/CDX4 (caudal-type homeobox) are higher in the Stomach column than in the Intestine column. In axolotl, is CDX expression… hmm. FABP6 peaks in Cloaca (8.6).\n\nHmm, hold on. Let me step back and think about which organs could have been swapped such that everything fits:\n\nCandidate: Stomach ↔ Intestine?\n- Intestine markers (CDX2/4, FABP2/6, VIL1, MUC2, ALPI, ANPEP, REG4, GUCY2C, SLC9A3, SLC26A3): in Stomach column 0.60 > Intestine 0.27 ✓ direction.\n- Stomach markers (PGC, MUC5AC×, TFF2, MUC4×): in Intestine column -0.19? No wait, Stomach markers in Intestine column = -0.11 (from T matrix: Stomach row, Intestine column = -0.11). ✗. Doesn't fit. The stomach set is garbage though.\n\nCandidate: Bladder ↔ Gill?\n- Bladder markers in Gill ✓✓ (1.33, self 0.09).\n- Gill markers in Bladder ✗ (-0.20). Gill set is weak.\n\nCandidate: Kidney ↔ Gill?\n- Kidney markers in Gill 0.88 vs self 0.19 ✓.\n- Gill markers in Kidney 0.06 ✗.\n\nCandidate: Cloaca ↔ Stomach (top-scoring pair!):\n- Cloaca markers (HOXC13, CDX2, CDX4, UPK3B, KRT13) in Stomach 0.79 vs self 0.24 ✓. Driven by CDX2 (Stomach 4.0 vs Cloaca 0.7!), CDX4 (Stomach 3.7 vs Cloaca 1.3), UPK3B (Stomach 1.1 vs 0.5), KRT13 (Prostate/GallBladder…). Hmm, CDX2 in stomach?? Biologically weird (CDX is intestinal/caudal).\n- Stomach markers in Cloaca -0.11 ✗.\n\nHmm, none of these are cleanly reciprocal with my current sets. My marker sets are contaminated by axolotl paralogs with non-specific profiles.\n\nLet me think differently. What if instead of trusting my marker sets, I use the data to find the most specific genes per organ and then interpret them with my biological knowledge? I.e., data-driven specificity + knowledge-based interpretation:\n\nFor each organ column c, find genes most specific to c (high z in c). Then check whether the top specific genes for column c match column c's label. If column \"Gill\"'s top specific genes are kidney genes (UMOD, SLC12A3, CUBN, LRP2…), then column Gill is actually kidney.\n\nThis is the reverse direction of what I did, and more robust: it uses all annotated genes with known function, not just my hand-picked (noisy) sets. Let me try it: rank genes by z[g,c], take top ~50 per column, list their [hs] symbols, and interpret.\n\nLet me generate that table. Then I can say: column X's top markers are actually markers of organ Y.\n\nAlso, do a correlation clustering of columns to see the relationships.\n\nLet me generate the top-specific genes per column.\n"}}
