{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_48","type":"reasoning","text":"Now the true picture is coming into view. Let me try to interpret the column-specific gene lists:\n\n- **Brain**: CDK5R2, SLC6A1, GRIN1, NEFL, GABRB3, KCNC1, OPRM1, HCN4, NTM, GRID1, NSG1, STMN2 — clearly neuronal ✓ Brain label OK.\n- **Heart**: MYH7B, ACTC1, TTN, MYBPC3, TRDN, HSPB7, LDB3, HAND2 — cardiac muscle ✓ Heart OK.\n- **Liver**: FETUB, FGA, F2, G6PC, KNG1, ITIH3, AHSG, SERPINA1, GYS2, CES1, CYP2A6/2C18, GPLD1, ALB? (not in top but FETUB/FGA/F2 are hepatic) ✓ Liver OK.\n- **Pancreas**: CTRB1/2, CPB1, PRSS1/2/3, CPA1/2, CELA2A, AMY2A, CTRL, MNX1 — pancreatic digestive enzymes ✓ Pancreas OK.\n- **Lung**: SFTPA2B, SFTPD, MUC5B ✓ Lung OK (plus HBG1/HBD/ALAS2 = blood-related; no problem).\n- **Intestine**: ALPI, SLC15A1, MGAM, APOA4, NXPE4, CCL25, OLFM4, DGAT2, FGL1, MYO1B, CEACAM1, NR5A2 — brush border/intestinal ✓ Intestine OK.\n- **Kidney**: SLC22A8, SLC22A6, SLC47A1, SLC2A11, UGT2A2?, SLC7A7, SLC13A1, SLC7A9, ABCG2, HAO2, GRHPR — proximal tubule transporters ✓ Kidney OK.\n- **Spleen**: IRF4, IGLV, GPR35, CD5L, CYTIP, GRAP2, CTSW, CCL5, RASGRP1, DOCK2, KLRF1 — immune/lymphoid ✓ Spleen OK.\n- **Limb**: TYR, DCT (melanocyte!), KRT6A, KRT12, THBS4, PRRX1, A2M, TGM5 — skin/pigment/limb ✓ reasonable (axolotl limb skin includes melanophores; PRRX1 is limb mesenchyme) ✓ Limb OK.\n\nNow the suspicious ones:\n\n- **Gill column**: UMOD×6 (!!), KRT4, KRT76, KRT15, KRT19, AQP4, SLC2A9, GP2, FCGBP, MUC4, MMP13, HAS1, TXNRD1, LRAT… **UMOD (uromodulin) is a highly specific kidney marker** (Tamm-Horsfall protein, thick ascending limb). There are 6 UMOD paralogs at the top of the Gill column! Plus KRT4 (urothelial/stratified epithelium), AQP4, SLC2A9 (uric acid transporter, kidney), GP2 (also pancreas/kidney?). This strongly suggests the Gill column is actually **kidney** (and maybe also bladder-like: KRT4?).\n\n  But wait — the Kidney column itself also looks kidney-like: SLC22A6/8, SLC47A1, SLC13A1, SLC7A9, HAO2 — proximal tubule. Hmm, both columns look kidney-like?! UMOD = thick ascending limb/distal; SLC22 = proximal tubule. Both are kidney sub-compartments!\n\n  Hmm, hold on. Maybe the Gill column is KIDNEY and the Kidney column is… also kidney? That would mean two kidney samples — unlikely. Let me reconsider: maybe Gill column = BLADDER? Bladder urothelium: KRT4, KRT19, UPK3B… and UMOD passes through the urinary tract… UMOD is kidney-specific (produced in TAL), but maybe its promoters are accessible in urinary tract cells? No — UMOD expression is kidney TAL-specific.\n\n  Hmm, but wait: in the Bladder column's top list there's: TUBB4B, MUC5AC, VENTX, AVPR2, MYOCD, CCR4, TMPRSS13, CASR, DMBT1… VENTX? MYOCD (myocardin — smooth muscle)? AVPR2 (vasopressin receptor 2 — kidney collecting duct!)… CASR (calcium-sensing receptor — kidney/parathyroid)! Hmm, the Bladder column has AVPR2, CASR — kidney-related genes! And MYOCD (bladder smooth muscle). Mixed.\n\n- **Cloaca column**: CLDN18, GKN1, CHIA×4, CNFN, MUC4, SULT6B1, CCL19, CCKAR, MNX1, ZP2… GKN1 (gastrokine 1 — stomach!), CLDN18 (stomach-type claudin!), CHIA (chitinase — gastric), CCKAR (CCK receptor — stomach/pancreas), MNX1 (pancreas). This looks like **stomach/pancreas/intestinal**… GKN1 + CLDN18 + CHIA strongly suggests **stomach**!\n\n- **Stomach column**: ANGPTL1, ANGPTL2, CD8B(!), TIMP1, MSMB(!), PRF1(!), FMO1, UGT1A1, DRD1, S100P, TMPRSS3, FUT2… MSMB (prostate!), CD8B/PRF1 (immune)… MSMB in the top list of the Stomach column. Hmm. And in the **Prostate column**: FCGBP, TRIM39, TRIM69, TRIM11, DMBT1×5, SSUH2, CARD6, CYP2J2, SLC27A1, PLA2G2D, PNMT, VMO1, HOXB9, LHX1, RXFP1, BMPR1B… DMBT1, FCGBP (mucins/immune), VMO1… hmm, VMO1 (vitelline membrane outer layer 1 homolog) — hmm. LHX1 (kidney development!), HOXB9… this doesn't really look like prostate.\n\n- **Bladder column**: TUBB4B, METTL21C, GIN1, B4GALNT2, STAR, TIMP4, MUC5AC, VENTX, KAZALD1, MAS1, MCM6, DMBT1, CYP2A7, CASR, ROBO4, CD163L1, AVPR2, CCR4, NDNF, PRSS27, TMPRSS13, CYP2A13, CDH13, MYOCD, CLEC2B, CRYGB… CASR, AVPR2 (kidney), MYOCD (smooth muscle)… mixed kidney/smooth muscle signal? STAR (steroidogenesis), VENTX… hmm.\n\nHmm, wait. Let me reconsider the Gill column once more: UMOD×6, KRT4, KRT15, KRT76, AQP4, SLC2A9, GP2. And the Kidney column: SLC22A6/A8, SLC47A1, SLC2A11, SLC13A1, SLC7A9, HAO2, GRHPR, ABCG2, UGT2A2.\n\nHmm, what if: **Gill column = true kidney**, **Kidney column = true gill**? But the Kidney column's top genes look kidney-like (proximal tubule transporters)… unless the \"true gill\" profile also includes those transporters? Gill ionocytes express SLC transporters, but SLC22A6/A8 (OAT1/OAT3), SLC47A1 (MATE1), SLC2A11, HAO2, UGT — these are renal/hepatic detox genes, not gill genes.\n\nAlternative: **Gill column = kidney**, **Kidney column = liver?** No, the Liver column is clearly liver.\n\nHmm, hold on. Let me reconsider: maybe both are kidney and the swap is **Gill ↔ Kidney**, and the reason both columns look kidney-like is that my \"top genes per column\" are picking up kidney markers in both because… no wait, if Kidney column = true gill, then the gill profile should show gill-specific genes (ion transporters like SLC4A4, carbonic anhydrase, Rhesus, etc.) at the top, not OATs.\n\nLet me look at the Kidney column's list once more: HSD17B11, AKR1A1, SLC22A8, SLC2A11, UGT2A2, SLC22A6, SLC47A1, TSPAN1, METTL7A, ANTKMT, SLC7A7, SLC26A2, ARSD, GRHPR, ABCG2, ADIF2(?), SLC13A1, TRIM4, PEG10, HAO2, NUP85, FRS2, MAN2B1, SLC7A9, ACAD11… These are mostly proximal tubule/kidney. So the Kidney column is kidney.\n\nThen why do 6 UMOD genes + KRT4 + AQP4 + SLC2A9 peak in the Gill column? UMOD is TAL-specific. SLC2A9 (GLUT9) — urate reabsorption, proximal tubule. AQP4 — hmm, AQP4 is brain (astrocyte) but also kidney collecting duct? KRT4 — urothelium/stratified epithelium. KRT15/KRT76 — epithelial. FCGBP — mucin-binding, immune. GP2 — glycoprotein of Paneth cells/pancreas…\n\nOh wait. Hmm, what if the Gill column were actually **bladder/urothelium**? KRT4, KRT15, UPK3B (bladder marker peaks in Gill!), KRT76 (differentiation of keratinized epithelium)… and UMOD — hmm, UMOD shouldn't be there. But hold on: maybe the \"UMOD [hs]\" annotation in axolotl is misassigned (UMOD is a zona pellucida domain protein; the ZP domain family also includes ZP2/ZP3 — the oocyte zona pellucida proteins!). The Cloaca column has ZP2! And the Lung column has ZP3! Hmm, interesting: ZP domain proteins. If these axolotl \"UMOD\" genes are actually other ZP domain proteins expressed in oviduct/egg jelly… the axolotl Gill sample — hmm.\n\nHold on, let me check what those UMOD genes actually are in the GTF (full gene_name, e.g., \"ZP-X [nr]|UMOD [hs]\"). Let me look at those specific genes. And GP2, KRT4, etc. too.\n\nActually, hold on — another thought. The axolotl is neotenic (retains larval characteristics): the Gill sample is from a larval gill. Axolotl larvae have pronephros/mesonephros… no wait. Hmm.\n\nLet me check whether UMOD-like genes in the Gill column are really annotated as UMOD, and also look at their positions. And — a clearer approach: check whether the Gill column resembles the Kidney column genome-wide (correlation). If Gill≈Kidney in profile, and both are… that would suggest duplication, not a swap.\n\nHmm, hold on. Let me re-examine the Bladder column's top list: CASR, AVPR2 — these are kidney collecting duct genes. MYOCD — smooth muscle (bladder wall). CCR4, CD163L1 — immune. VENTX — homeobox (axolotl ventral?). STAR — steroid. Hmm.\n\nNow let me think again about the **Cloaca column**: GKN1, CLDN18, CHIA×4, MUC4, CNFN, CCKAR, MNX1, SULT6B1, CYP1A1… GKN1 + CLDN18 + CHIA = stomach surface mucous cells. CCKAR = stomach/pancreas/intestine. MNX1 = pancreas. Hmm, so the Cloaca column looks stomach + pancreas-like?? And the Stomach column: MSMB (prostate!), CD8B, PRF1, ANGPTL1/2, TIMP1/3, FMO1, S100P, TMPRSS3, DRD1, UGT1A1, NXPE3, HOXC6, DSG4, PADI1… S100P — hmm (pancreatic/biliary?), NXPE3 — hmm.\n\nHmm, what if the swap were **Cloaca ↔ Stomach** (the top-ranked pair in the first normalized run, score 0.749)? Evidence: Cloaca markers (CDX2/CDX4) peak in the Stomach column; and Stomach column… GKN1/CLDN18/CHIA (stomach genes!) peak in the Cloaca column!! Let me verify: GKN1 in the Cloaca column is 3.7 — where else is GKN1 high? CHIA in Cloaca 3.7. Let me check their full profiles. If GKN1/CLDN18/CHIA/PGC are stomach-specific in this dataset and peak in the Cloaca column, and CDX2/CDX4 (caudal/intestinal… hmm, cloacal) peak in the Stomach column…\n\nHmm wait, CDX2/CDX4 — CDX is a caudal homeobox gene. The cloaca is literally the caudal-most region! CDX2 is expressed in posterior/caudal endoderm — so CDX2/CDX4 high = caudal identity = cloaca ✓. In the earlier detail: CDX2: Stomach 4.0, Intestine 3.5, Cloaca 0.7. CDX4: Stomach 3.7, Intestine 2.4, Cloaca 1.3. So CDX2/CDX4 (caudal/cloacal markers) peak in the Stomach column!\n\nAnd stomach genes (GKN1, CLDN18, CHIA, PGC?, MUC5AC?) peak in the Cloaca column? Let me check the full profiles of GKN1, CLDN18, CHIA, PGC, MUC5AC, TFF2.\n\nThis fits a **Stomach ↔ Cloaca swap** perfectly:\n- Stomach column contains the cloacal profile → CDX2/CDX4 (caudal markers) high ✓.\n- Cloaca column contains the stomach profile → GKN1/CLDN18/CHIA high ✓ (need to verify the profiles are actually stomach-specific).\n\nLet me verify by checking the full profiles of GKN1, CLDN18, CHIA, PGC, MUC5AC, TFF2, MSMB, and also what else peaks specifically in the Stomach column that shouldn't be there (CD8B/PRF1 immune — hmm, maybe due to MALT/immune infiltration in cloaca? Cloaca has lymphoid tissue!). Actually interesting: Stomach column top = CD8B, PRF1 — immune genes. The Cloaca contains immune tissue (cloacal bursa/BALT in birds…). Hmm, if the Stomach column = cloaca tissue, it would contain lymphoid cells → CD8B/PRF1 ✓ makes sense! And MSMB? Hmm, MSMB in cloaca? Unclear.\n\nAnd ANGPTL1/ANGPTL2, TIMP1/3, FMO1 — hmm.\n\nOK, so now let me systematically verify: for a set of candidate marker genes, output full profiles:\n- Stomach genes: GKN1, CLDN18, CHIA, PGC, MUC5AC, TFF2, ATP4A (absent), GIF (absent), LIPF (absent), PGA (absent), SLC… also NXPE3? S100P?\n- Cloaca/caudal genes: CDX2, CDX4, HOXC13, UPK3B, KRT13, FABP6 (peaked in Cloaca!), SLC26A9 (peaked in Cloaca!).\n- Gill/kidney: UMOD, KRT4, AQP4, SLC2A9, GP2.\n\nFrom the earlier detail dump: FABP6: Cloaca 8.6 (!), Stomach 7.3, Intestine 7.2. SLC26A9: Cloaca 3.0, Intestine 1.6, Lung 1.0. UPK3B: Gill 2.4, Stomach 1.1.\n\nHmm, FABP6 peaks in Cloaca — so the Cloaca column has genuine caudal markers?? But I hypothesized that Cloaca column = stomach profile. Contradiction? Unless FABP6 is really an intestinal marker (ileal FABP6), and the true cloaca includes intestine-like tissue (cloaca includes the terminal intestine). If Cloaca column = true cloaca, then FABP6/SLC26A9 high is expected ✓. Hmm!\n\nWait wait. Let me redo this. If the Cloaca column is truly cloaca, then GKN1/CLDN18/CHIA high in the Cloaca column means… GKN1/CLDN18/CHIA are stomach genes, and cloaca shouldn't express them. Unless the axolotl Cloaca sample includes stomach tissue?? No…\n\nHmm, or GKN1/CLDN18/CHIA are not stomach-specific in this dataset. Let me check their full profiles before concluding. Similarly, UMOD's full profile: if UMOD peaks only in Gill and Kidney column UMOD is low… hmm.\n\nOK, so now let me output full profiles (all 15 columns) for a curated list of genes: GKN1, CLDN18, CHIA, CDX2, CDX4, HOXC13, UPK3B, KRT4, KRT13, FABP6, SLC26A9, UMOD, AQP4, SLC2A9, GP2, PGC, MUC5AC, TFF2, MSMB, CD8B, PRF1, ALPI, MGAM, APOA4, RHAG, CA4, CA5B, SFTPA2B, CTRB1, PRSS1, AMY2A, SLC22A6, SLC47A1, UGT2A2, MYH7B, ACTC1, GRIN1, NEFL, SLC6A1, TUBB3, FGA, G6PC, TYR, DCT, IRF4."}}
