CASR     | Stomach:nan, Spleen:nan, Prostate:nan, Pancreas:nan, Lung:nan\r\n\r\nMYOCD    | Bladder:1.7, Stomach:1.7, GallBladder:1.3, Heart:1.2, Gill:1.2\r\n\r\nSTAR     | Bladder:1.8, Lung:1.4, Prostate:1.1, Cloaca:0.9, Liver:0.7\r\n\r\nVENTX    | Heart:0.8, GallBladder:0.8, Lung:0.7, Bladder:0.7, Pancreas:0.7\r\nVENTX    | Kidney:0.5, Heart:0.5, Brain:0.5, GallBladder:0.5, Pancreas:0.5\r\nVENTX    | Bladder:1.6, Prostate:1.1, Heart:1.1, Gill:0.9, Brain:0.8\r\n\r\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_51","type":"reasoning","text":"The full profiles are very informative. Main findings:\n\n**Cloaca column = stomach profile:**\n- GKN1 (gastrokine-1, stomach-specific): copy peaks at Cloaca 7.1 (Stomach only 0.7!)\n- CLDN18 (stomach claudin): Cloaca 8.5 vs everything else ≤1.3 (!!!)\n- CHIA copies: Cloaca 6.9-7.0 (chitinase is a gastric enzyme)\n- FABP6: Cloaca 8.6 (hmm — FABP6… this is odd; maybe a different annotation)\n- SLC26A9: Cloaca 3.0\n\nGKN1 + CLDN18 + CHIA are classic stomach markers, and they strongly peak in the Cloaca column. So the sample labeled Cloaca has stomach chromatin.\n\n**Stomach column = cloaca (caudal) profile:**\n- CDX2 (caudal homeobox): Stomach 4.0 > Intestine 3.5 > Cloaca 0.7\n- CDX4: Stomach 3.7 (copy 1); the other copy peaks at Prostate/Cloaca 3.5/3.1\n- KRT19 copy: Stomach 4.4, Cloaca 4.1 (hmm, both)\n- Also REG4: Stomach 2.4 (REG4 is intestinal-type…). Hmm.\n\nHmm, wait, but is CDX2/CDX4 really a \"cloaca\" marker? CDX genes are posterior/caudal markers, and in the context of this organ set, the most caudal organ = Cloaca. CDX2 is also a classic intestinal marker (in humans, CDX2 is a master regulator of the intestine). In mice, Cdx2 is expressed in the intestinal epithelium. So CDX2 peaks in the Stomach column and is high in the Intestine column… this could mean Stomach column = Intestine! And the Intestine column = Stomach? Let's check that alternative: Stomach↔Intestine.\n\nEvidence for Stomach column = Intestine:\n- CDX2/CDX4 high ✓ (intestinal markers)\n- But the Intestine column has its own intestinal markers: ALPI 3.7, MGAM 3.7, APOA4 3.7, SLC15A1 3.7, NXPE4 3.7, CCL25 3.5, OLFM4 3.2, DGAT2 3.4 — clearly intestinal ✓. If Intestine↔Stomach were swapped, the Intestine column should contain the stomach profile, but — instead it has the intestinal profile. So Intestine column = true intestine. ✓ No swap there.\n\nTherefore: the Stomach column has CDX2/CDX4 (caudal markers) → true cloaca; the Cloaca column has GKN1/CLDN18/CHIA (stomach markers) → true stomach. A mutually consistent story: **Stomach ↔ Cloaca swap**.\n\nLet's double-check the \"caudal\" interpretation of CDX2: in this dataset, which column should CDX2 peak in? Options: Intestine (intestinal identity) or Cloaca (most caudal). CDX2: Stomach 4.0, Intestine 3.5, Cloaca 0.7. If labels were correct, CDX2 should peak at Intestine (3.5) — close to Stomach's 4.0. Hmm, ambiguous on its own. But combined with CDX4 (Stomach 3.7, Intestine 2.4) and KRT19 copy (Stomach 4.4, Cloaca 4.1) and REG4 (Stomach 2.4)… hmm, wait, KRT19 peaks in both Stomach and Cloaca. And CASR copy: Cloaca 2.7, Prostate 2.5, Stomach 2.2.\n\nHmm, but wait — GKN1/CLDN18 in the Cloaca column is a very clean signal (CLDN18: Cloaca 8.5, next is 1.3!). CLDN18 (claudin-18) is a well-known stomach-specific tight junction protein (a gastric cancer marker). GKN1 (gastrokine 1) is stomach-specific. CHIA (acidic mammalian chitinase) is a stomach enzyme (gastric chitinase). These three genes strongly mark the stomach. They peak in the Cloaca column. So the Cloaca column = stomach sample.\n\nNext, conversely: is there stomach tissue in the Stomach column? Stomach column top: ANGPTL1, CD8B, ANGPTL2, TIMP1, TMPRSS3, MAST4, MSMB, FMO1, PRF1, TIMP3, FUT2, RDH16, DRD1, S100P, NXPE3, UGT1A1, AOX1, CYP4A11, HOXC6, DSG4, PADI1, MYH8, GLT1D1, RTP1, ACO2, AIFM3, FOXD1, TRIM2, RNASEH1, SUGT1P4-STRA6LP, HSA6077, ERV3-2, C16ORF89/87, PGAM2, APEH, S100P…\n\nHmm, what organ has CD8B, PRF1 (cytotoxic T cells), IRF4 (lymphocytes), MSMB, ANGPTL1/2, TIMP1/3, FMO1, TMPRSS3, MAST4, NXPE3, S100P, DRD1, HOXC6, DSG4, PADI1?\n\nHmm… MSMB + TMPRSS3 + HOXC6… prostate-ish? MSMB (β-microseminoprotein) is prostate-specific; TMPRSS3 is… hmm, TMPRSS3 is expressed in the ovary and inner ear; HOXC6 — hmm. DSG4 (desmoglein-4) — skin/hair. PADI1 — skin (peptidylarginine deiminase, keratinization). Hmm, DSG4 + PADI1 + KRT76 (in the Gill column)…\n\nWait, let me look at the Prostate column's top genes again: FCGBP, TRIM39, TRIM69, TRIM11, DMBT1×5, SSUH2, CARD6, CYP2J2, SLC27A1, PLA2G2D, PNMT, NNMT, TCF15, VMO1, PLEKHB1, HOXB9, UNC13A, PLAC8L1, INPP5A, CYP4V2, MCTP2, FAM167A, RPL8, KCNK5, RXFP1, BMPR1B, KCNJ12, LHX1, MSI2, CENPJ, P2RY2.\n\nLHX1: Prostate 7.1 (!), Kidney 3.0. HOXB9: Prostate 2.6. Hmm.\n\nAnd the Gill column: UMOD copies (Gill 3.7-6.7!), AQP4 (Gill 5.8!), KRT4 (Gill 3.7), KRT76 3.7, KRT15 3.6, KRT19 3.4, UPK3B (Gill 2.4), SLC2A9 copy (Gill 1.4-1.5), VIL1 copy (Gill 4.8!), TXNRD1, FCGBP, UCP2, PLAUR, ACSBG2, LRAT, ATP13A5, CDH26, TECTA, HAS1, NNMT, MMP13, CXADR, MUC4, NKPD1, CD274, DCSTAMP, ST8SIA5, GLUL, LYPD2, ABCA12.\n\nHmm, KRT4 + KRT76 + KRT15 + KRT19 + UPK3B — urothelial/epithelial; AQP4 Gill 5.8; VIL1 Gill 4.8. UMOD copies peak massively at Gill.\n\nHmm, wait — what is the Gill column actually? Let me think about the organs that express UMOD + AQP4 + KRT4 + VIL1 + UPK3B + SLC2A9… UMOD = kidney TAL. AQP4 = kidney collecting duct (and brain astrocytes, lung). KRT4 = urothelium. UPK3B = urothelium. VIL1 = brush border (intestine/kidney proximal tubule!). SLC2A9 = kidney proximal tubule (urate). So the Gill column has a mix of kidney (UMOD, SLC2A9, VIL1) + urothelium (KRT4, UPK3B) + AQP4. This is a urinary tract signature: kidney + bladder/urothelium!\n\nInteresting. So Gill column ≈ kidney + urothelium?? Hmm. Or maybe the Gill sample is actually the **Bladder** (urothelium: KRT4, UPK3B, KRT15, KRT76 ✓; and UMOD — uromodulin passes through the urine; is UMOD's promoter accessible in the bladder? Unlikely…).\n\nHmm, or maybe Gill column = **Kidney**, and the kidney sample contains renal pelvis urothelium (KRT4/UPK3B — renal pelvis is lined by urothelium!), and AQP4 (collecting duct), VIL1 (proximal tubule brush border), SLC2A9 (proximal tubule), UMOD (TAL). Yes! A bulk kidney sample would include renal pelvis urothelium → KRT4/UPK3B accessible. This fits perfectly: Gill column = kidney.\n\nThen what about the Kidney column? Its top genes: SLC22A8, SLC22A6, SLC47A1, SLC2A11, SLC7A7, SLC13A1, SLC7A9, HAO2, GRHPR, ABCG2, UGT2A2, AKR1A1, HSD17B11, METTL7A, SLC26A2, MAN2B1, ACAD11, ACOT12, FMO2 — these are also kidney (proximal tubule transporters and metabolic enzymes)! SLC22A6=OAT1, SLC22A8=OAT3, SLC47A1=MATE1, SLC13A1=NaDC1, SLC7A9=b0,+AT (kidney), HAO2 (glycolate oxidase, liver and kidney), GRHPR (liver/kidney). Hmm, so the Kidney column also looks kidney-like.\n\nTwo kidney-like columns?! This suggests that Gill column = kidney and Kidney column = kidney — no wait. Unless the Kidney column is actually **liver**? HAO2, GRHPR, AKR1A1, ABCG2, UGT2A2, FMO2 are also hepatic… but SLC22A6/A8, SLC47A1, SLC13A1, SLC7A9 are renal transporters. And the Liver column is clearly liver (FGA, F2, G6PC, KNG1, ITIH3, AHSG, GPLD1, FETUB, SERPINA1, GYS2, CES1).\n\nHmm, hmm. Wait, maybe in axolotl the liver and kidney share more… no, that's not right.\n\nLet me reconsider: maybe the Gill column isn't kidney. The UMOD paralogs: 22 UMOD [hs] genes in axolotl! Mammals have one UMOD. These are probably ZP domain gene family members (UMOD, ZP1, ZP2, ZP3, ZP4, ZPD, ZPAX, glycoprotein hormones, etc. are all ZP domain proteins). The axolotl ZP family may have expanded. ZP genes are expressed in the oviduct/egg envelope — but Gill? Hmm. However, some copies peak in Gill with z 3.5-3.7 and CPM 3.7-6.7, with the second column far lower (Gill 6.7 vs Stomach 3.4; Gill 3.9 vs Bladder 1.1; Gill 3.7 vs Cloaca 1.7).\n\nHmm, wait — let me actually look at the UMOD profile again:\n- UMOD: Gill 6.7, Stomach 3.4, Bladder 2.2, Intestine 2.0, Limb 2.0\n- UMOD: Gill 3.9, Prostate 2.1, Stomach 1.7, Cloaca 1.6\n- UMOD: Gill 3.7, Bladder 2.4, Lung 1.7\n- UMOD: Gill 3.7, Cloaca 1.7, Stomach 1.5, Bladder 1.4\n- UMOD: Gill 3.9, Bladder 1.1\n- UMOD: Gill 3.4, Heart 1.5\n- UMOD: Gill 2.6, Prostate 0.8\n- UMOD: Gill 2.4, Heart 1.4\n- UMOD: Gill 2.3, Limb 0.7\n- UMOD: Gill 2.1, Intestine 0.8\n- UMOD: Spleen 2.7, Gill 1.8\n- UMOD: Prostate 1.8, Gill 1.4\n- UMOD: Heart 1.3, Gill 1.2, Kidney 1.2\n- UMOD: Kidney 0.9, Heart 0.9 (ubiquitous)\n- UMOD: Brain 0.7, Spleen 0.7 (ubiquitous)\n\nSo about 12 UMOD-family genes peak in Gill (2.1-6.7), with Kidney column low (0.5-1.2). If these are true UMOD orthologs (TAL), the Gill column should be kidney — but in that case, the Kidney column (SLC22A6 etc.) would be kidney too. Contradiction → probably these are ZP domain paralogs with different annotation assignments, not true UMOD. In fact, in axolotl, the kidney is mesonephric; hmm.\n\nOK, another thought — what if the Gill column were actually **Lung**? No, the Lung column has SFTPA2B/SFTPD/MUC5B.\n\nWhat if the Gill column were actually **GallBladder**? No, KRT4/UPK3B aren't biliary.\n\nHmm, let me look at the Bladder column's top genes once more: TUBB4B, METTL21C, HSA6077, GIN1, B4GALNT2, STAR, TIMP4, MUC5AC, HAQPCD, VENTX, KAZALD1, MAS1, MCM6, DMBT1, CYP2A7, CASR, ROBO4, CD163L1, HCG27957, ERV3-2, RTL1, AVPR2, CCR4, NDNF, PRSS27, TMPRSS13, CYP2A13, CDH13, HERC3, MYOCD, CLEC2B, CYP2F1, MEGF6, BIRC5, CRYGB, C1QTNF2, DLG2, CMKLR1.\n\nAVPR2 (vasopressin V2 receptor — kidney collecting duct!), CASR (parathyroid/kidney), MYOCD (smooth muscle — bladder wall!), STAR, MCM6 (proliferation), CCR4/CD163L1 (immune). Hmm, AVPR2 + CASR + MYOCD — kidney collecting duct + smooth muscle. This is… hmm, actually AVPR2+CASR is collecting duct/kidney. If the Bladder column is actually kidney (collecting duct + smooth muscle of renal pelvis/ureter)…\n\nHmm, wait a moment, wait. Let me step back and think:\n\n- Gill column: UMOD-family (TAL?), VIL1 (proximal tubule brush border), SLC2A9 (proximal tubule), AQP4, KRT4/UPK3B (urothelium of renal pelvis), KRT76/15/19 → kidney signature ✓✓\n- Kidney column: SLC22A6/A8 (OAT1/3), SLC47A1 (MATE1), SLC13A1, SLC7A9, HAO2, GRHPR → kidney proximal tubule signature ✓\n- Bladder column: AVPR2, CASR, MYOCD → collecting duct + smooth muscle… also kidney-like?? Hmm.\n\nThree kidney-like columns?? That can't be. Something is off with my interpretation. Let me reconsider.\n\nHmm, hmm. Wait. Maybe the problem is different: maybe the Gill column really is gill, and gill expresses these genes? Axolotl gills: respiratory epithelium with ionocytes. UMOD-family ZP proteins? In fish, ZP genes are expressed in skin/mucus? Actually, in fish, zona pellucida genes are expressed in the skin/epidermis of embryos/larvae (egg envelope proteins are also expressed in larval skin)! In medaka and zebrafish, ZP genes are expressed in the skin epithelium. Axolotl larvae have skin… and gills are epithelial-rich. KRT4/KRT76/KRT15/KRT19 — keratins of gill epithelium ✓. AQP4 — gill osmoregulation ✓ plausible. VIL1 — hmm.\n\nUgh, this is really ambiguous. The \"UMOD [hs]\" annotation is probably a ZP family member expressed in larval skin/gill epithelium. So the Gill column may really be gill.\n\nOK, let me refocus. The strongest and cleanest anomaly signals I have are:\n\n1. **CLDN18**: Cloaca 8.5, everything else ≤1.3. CLDN18 is one of the most stomach-specific genes known.\n2. **GKN1** copy: Cloaca 7.1, next is 1.8. Gastrokine — stomach-specific.\n3. **CHIA** copy: Cloaca 6.9-7.0. Gastric chitinase. (Also CHIA in the Pancreas column 1.9/1.4 — hmm.)\n4. **CDX2**: Stomach 4.0 > Intestine 3.5 > Cloaca 0.7; **CDX4**: Stomach 3.7 > Intestine 2.4 > Cloaca 1.3.\n5. **KRT19** copy: Stomach 4.4, Cloaca 4.1 — hmm, high in both; not discriminating.\n6. **FABP6**: Cloaca 8.6, Stomach 7.3, Intestine 7.2, Liver 6.5, Spleen 6.2 — broadly high, not discriminating (probably a different gene family).\n\nHmm wait, #4 is tricky. If Cloaca↔Stomach are swapped:\n- Stomach column (= true cloaca): CDX2/CDX4 high ✓ plausible (caudal).\n- Cloaca column (= true stomach): CLDN18/GKN1/CHIA high ✓✓✓ strong.\n- Stomach column (= true cloaca): stomach markers should be low → PGC in Stomach column: 4.7?! Hmm: PGC profile: Liver 5.5, Cloaca 5.5, Stomach 4.7, Spleen 4.2, Intestine 4.1 — PGC is high almost everywhere?? Not discriminating (a bad PGC annotation, or pepsinogen is broadly accessible?). Actually, PGC is 4.7 in Stomach column — if Stomach column were true cloaca, PGC should be low there. 4.7 is moderately high. Hmm. But PGC is also 5.5 in Liver, 4.2 in Spleen, 4.1 in Intestine — so this \"PGC\" gene is not stomach-specific here (a misannotated paralog). So ignore PGC.\n\n- Cloaca column (= true stomach): CDX2 should be low → CDX2 in Cloaca = 0.7 ✓ low! CDX4 in Cloaca = 1.3 ✓ lowish.\n- Stomach column (= true cloaca): CLDN18 should be low → CLDN18 in Stomach column: not in top 5; need value. Let me get the full profile of CLDN18: Cloaca 8.5, Limb 1.3, Gill 1.2, Heart 1.2, Bladder 1.2 → Stomach <1.2 ✓ low.\n\nSo the CLDN18/GKN1/CHIA/CDX2/CDX4 pattern is fully consistent with a Stomach↔Cloaca swap, with no contradictions except the weakly discriminating ones (KRT19, PGC, FABP6).\n\nAdditional check — the Stomach column's immune genes (CD8B 3.7, PRF1 3.5, IRF4 2.8): cloaca contains cloacal-associated lymphoid tissue. In birds/reptiles, the cloacal bursa is a major immune organ. In amphibians too, cloacal lymphoid tissue exists. So CD8B/PRF1/IRF4 high in the true cloaca sample is biologically plausible ✓. Hmm, interesting: IRF4 profile: Spleen 3.2, Stomach 2.8, Cloaca 2.4 — spleen (lymphoid) highest, then Stomach column. If Stomach column = cloaca (lymphoid-rich), IRF4 2.8 makes sense.\n\nAnd the Cloaca column's MUC4 copy: MUC4 3.6 in the top of the Cloaca column — MUC4 is a gastric surface mucin (also trachea/intestine). ✓ Consistent with stomach.\n\nAlso the Cloaca column: CCKAR 3.3 (CCK-A receptor — stomach/pancreas/gallbladder), MNX1 3.3 (hmm, MNX1 is pancreatic motor neuron…), SULT6B1, CYP1A1, CCL19 3.5, ZP2 3.2, GPR39 3.2 (Zn receptor, gastric?), ENTPD8, MFAP4, LMO4, BMP1, FRRS1, MARVELD2, ALOXE3, VSIG2, CLIC6, CNFN…\n\nHmm, GKN1/CLDN18/CHIA/MUC4/CCKAR/GPR39 all point to stomach. Good.\n\nSo now, what about the Gill/Kidney/Bladder confusion? Let me re-examine whether there's a second anomaly — but the task says \"two organ labels\" (one pair). The Gill column's signature (UMOD-family, KRT4, AQP4, VIL1) — if the Gill column really is gill (ZP/keratin expression in larval gill epithelium), everything is consistent, and the only swap is Stomach↔Cloaca.\n\nHmm, but wait — let me double-check the Kidney column and Bladder column: Kidney column's SLC22A6/A8 etc. = kidney ✓ consistent. Bladder column's MYOCD (smooth muscle), AVPR2/CASR — hmm, AVPR2 is kidney collecting duct; but it's also… AVPR2 in bladder? The bladder urothelium responds to vasopressin? Hmm, CASR is also in bladder urothelium. MYOCD = bladder detrusor smooth muscle ✓. DMBT1 (mucin) ✓. MUC5AC copy ✓. VENTX — homeobox… OK, the Bladder column is reasonably bladder-like.\n\nActually, wait — one more important check on the Stomach↔Cloaca hypothesis: in the T matrix from run 2, Cloaca markers (my set: HOXC13, CDX2, CDX4, UPK3B, KRT13) peaked at Stomach 0.79 ✓, and Stomach markers (PGC, MUC5AC copies, TFF2, MUC4 copies) peaked at… Heart 0.74, Cloaca -0.11 ✗. But my Stomach marker set was garbage (MUC5AC/PGC paralogs not specific). Better stomach markers: GKN1, CLDN18, CHIA — I wasn't using these! Let me add GKN1, CLDN18, CHIA, CCKAR, MUC4 (hmm), REG4? (REG4 peaks in Stomach column 2.4 — REG4 is intestinal, hmm, but in axolotl…).\n\nHmm wait, REG4: Stomach 2.4, Heart 1.6, Liver 1.5, Lung 1.4, Cloaca 1.4 — peaks in Stomach column. If Stomach column = true cloaca, REG4 (intestinal/colonic) being high there is… plausible? Cloaca includes the terminal hindgut ✓ REG4 (colonic) plausible ✓.\n\nLet me refine the marker sets based on these findings and re-run:\n- Stomach: add GKN1, CLDN18, CHIA, CCKAR, S100P? (S100P: stomach? in the top list of the Stomach column — if the Stomach column is really cloaca, S100P there isn't a stomach marker…). Be careful: only use genes whose profiles are stomach-specific under the swap hypothesis: GKN1 (Cloaca column), CLDN18 (Cloaca column), CHIA (Cloaca column), MUC4 copy (Cloaca column) — these are \"stomach genes found in the Cloaca column\". For the reverse direction (cloaca genes in the Stomach column): CDX2, CDX4, REG4?, CD8B?, PRF1?, IRF4? Hmm, CD8B/PRF1 are immune (cloacal lymphoid tissue), IRF4 is lymphoid.\n\n- Also remove contaminated markers: the MUC5AC copies from Stomach (not specific), PGC (not specific), MUC4 copies — check each: MUC4 copies: GallBladder 2.8, Cloaca 2.1, Intestine 2.0, Stomach 1.9, 1.4 — hmm, MUC4 is also not specific. Keep only GKN1, CLDN18, CHIA, CCKAR.\n\n- Cloaca: CDX2, CDX4, REG4, CD8B, PRF1, IRF4, FABP6?, SLC26A9?, KRT13? Hmm. Keep CDX2, CDX4, REG4, CD8B, PRF1, IRF4, SLC26A9, FABP6 — hmm, FABP6's profile is broad (Cloaca 8.6, Stomach 7.3, Intestine 7.2, Liver 6.5) — not specific. Drop FABP6. SLC26A9: Cloaca 3.0, Intestine 1.6 — semi-specific; keep.\n\nHmm wait, but note: if I pick \"cloaca markers\" based on their high values in the Stomach column, that's circular reasoning (data snooping). The honest approach: CDX2/CDX4 are established caudal/posterior markers (prior knowledge ✓), REG4 is a hindgut/colonic marker (prior ✓), CD8B/PRF1/IRF4 are lymphoid markers + knowledge that cloaca has lymphoid tissue (weak prior, plausible). GKN1/CLDN18/CHIA/CCKAR are established stomach markers (prior ✓). So the swap hypothesis is supported by prior knowledge genes, not data-driven selection. The data-driven exploration just identified which prior-knowledge genes have informative profiles.\n\nNow, what about the Gill column and the UMOD-family/AQP4/KRT4/VIL1 issue? Under the \"Gill column is true gill\" interpretation: larval gill epithelium expresses keratins (KRT4/15/19/76 ✓ plausible), ZP-family egg envelope/skin proteins (plausible in larval epithelium ✓), AQP4 (osmoregulation ✓), VIL1 (hmm). But the Kidney column has SLC22A6/A8/SLC47A1/SLC13A1/SLC7A9 — kidney ✓, and the Bladder column has MYOCD/AVPR2/CASR — bladder(-kidney)… if the Gill column is true gill, there's no second anomaly.\n\nHmm, wait. But actually, let me reconsider the possibility that the swap is Gill↔Kidney instead of Stomach↔Cloaca. Under Gill↔Kidney:\n- Gill column = true kidney: UMOD-family ✓ (if true UMOD), VIL1 ✓, SLC2A9 ✓, AQP4 ✓, KRT4/UPK3B (renal pelvis urothelium) ✓ — plausible.\n- Kidney column = true gill: SLC22A6/A8, SLC47A1, SLC13A1, SLC7A9, HAO2, UGT2A2, GRHPR… are these gill genes? SLC22/OAT — no, these are kidney-specific organic anion transporters. SLC7A9 (renal amino acid transporter; mutations cause cystinuria) — kidney. HAO2 — liver/kidney. So the Kidney column has a renal tubule signature. For Gill↔Kidney to hold, the Kidney column should have a gill signature — it doesn't. ✗✗. So Gill↔Kidney is excluded: the Kidney column really is kidney.\n\nSimilarly, Bladder↔Gill: the Bladder column should have a gill signature — it has MYOCD/AVPR2/CASR/DMBT1 — not gill. ✗.\n\nTherefore the only consistent swap candidate is Stomach↔Cloaca:\n- Cloaca column = stomach: GKN1, CLDN18, CHIA×4, MUC4, CCKAR, GPR39 ✓✓ (all stomach-specific prior-knowledge genes).\n- Stomach column = cloaca: CDX2, CDX4 (caudal markers), REG4 (hindgut), CD8B/PRF1/IRF4 (cloacal lymphoid tissue) ✓ plausible.\n\nMutual and consistent!\n\nHmm, but let me also reconsider Stomach↔Intestine once more: the Intestine column has ALPI/MGAM/APOA4/SLC15A1/OLFM4/CCL25/NXPE4 — clearly intestinal ✓ (not stomach). So Intestine column = true intestine ✓. And if Stomach column = true intestine, CDX2/CDX4 high ✓ would fit (CDX2 is intestinal!)… but in that case, where is the true stomach profile? It should be in the Intestine column — but the Intestine column is clearly intestinal, not stomach (no GKN1/CLDN18/CHIA: GKN1 in Intestine is 1.1, CLDN18 in Intestine is ~<1.3, CHIA in Intestine is low). ✗. So Stomach↔Intestine is excluded.\n\nAnd Cloaca↔Intestine: the Intestine column is truly intestinal ✓ (so Cloaca ≠ Intestine's partner).\n\nGreat — Stomach↔Cloaca is the unique candidate. This also matches run 2's pair ranking where Cloaca-Stomach was #3 (0.749) — with my noisy sets; with the corrected sets, it should dominate.\n\nAlso note from run 2: Bladder-Gill was #1 (1.090) — driven by UPK3B/KRT4 in Gill, which I now interpret as genuine gill/renal-pelvis-like expression… hmm, wait. UPK3B: Gill 2.4, Stomach 1.1, Limb 1.0, Bladder 0.9. KRT4: Gill 2.1, Limb 0.7. Hmm. UPK3B is urothelium-specific (prior knowledge). UPK3B being high in the Gill column is strange if Gill = gill. But it's a single gene, and the axolotl UPK3B annotation might also be a paralog issue. And KRT4 (Gill 2.1) — KRT4 is a mucosal/urothelial keratin. Hmm.\n\nHmm, let me think about whether Bladder↔Gill could be the swap:\n- Gill column = true bladder: KRT4 ✓, UPK3B ✓, KRT19 ✓, KRT15 ✓, KRT76 ✓, AQP4? (bladder? no), UMOD-family? (no), VIL1? (no), TXNRD1? FCGBP? Hmm. Bladder urothelium: UPKs, KRT4/13/20, PPARG, FOXA1… the UMOD-family/VIL1/SLC2A9 don't fit bladder.\n- Bladder column = true gill: MYOCD/AVPR2/CASR/DMBT1/MUC5AC/VENTX/STAR — not gill at all. ✗✗ Excluded.\n\nOK, so Bladder↔Gill is excluded in the reverse direction (Bladder column has no gill signature). Good.\n\nTherefore: **swap = Stomach ↔ Cloaca**, with strong evidence in the Cloaca→stomach direction (GKN1, CLDN18, CHIA) and supportive evidence in the Stomach→cloaca direction (CDX2, CDX4, REG4, lymphoid genes).\n\nHmm, another alternative for the Cloaca column's stomach signature: could the Cloaca sample be stomach+cloaca contamination? For a benchmark, a clean label swap is more likely.\n\nNow let me also double-check the other direction more rigorously: are there other \"stomach genes\" that peak in the Cloaca column? Let me check more stomach-related genes in the annotation: CHIT1? MUC1? ATP4A absent… Let me check what's available: 'GKN', 'CLDN', 'PGA', 'PGC', 'ATP', 'GAST', 'GIF', 'LIPF', 'TFF', 'CHIA', 'CHIT', 'CCK', 'GHRL', 'MUC'. And caudal/cloacal genes: 'CDX', 'HOX' (especially HOXA13/D13 absent), 'WNT', 'EVX' (EVX1/2 are posterior