{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_54","type":"reasoning","text":"Great, more evidence is coming together:\n\n- **CCKAR** (cholecystokinin A receptor — stomach): Cloaca 4.3, next is Brain 2.7. ✓ Stomach gene peaking in the Cloaca column.\n- **EVX2** (posterior/caudal homeobox): copy 2: Stomach 3.8, Bladder 3.5, Cloaca 3.2 — hmm, Stomach is top but Bladder is also high. Not a clean marker. Skip EVX2 (or treat as weak).\n- **ALPI** (intestinal alkaline phosphatase): copy 1: Stomach 4.5 (!), Cloaca 3.7, Intestine 2.5. Interesting — ALPI peaks in the Stomach column. ALPI is an intestinal marker; in the swap hypothesis (Stomach column = true cloaca), cloaca contains the terminal hindgut → ALPI expression is plausible ✓. But hmm, ALPI in stomach?? If it were gastric alkaline phosphatase… actually, there's also \"tissue-nonspecific AP.\" In the axolotl [hs] assignment, the ALPI copy might be an AP paralog. Keep as a secondary signal only.\n- **SLC26A9**: Cloaca 3.0, Intestine 1.6 — hmm, under the swap hypothesis, SLC26A9 peaks in the Cloaca column, but… wait, if the Cloaca column = true stomach, why is SLC26A9 (intestinal-type anion transporter) high there? Hmm. SLC26A9 is a gastric/bronchial anion channel! Actually, SLC26A9 is expressed in stomach (surface mucous cells), lung, and intestine. Yes — SLC26A9 is known as a gastric Cl− channel. ✓ Consistent with the Cloaca column being the true stomach.\n- **MSMB**: copy 1: Stomach 2.0, others ≤0.8. MSMB is prostate-specific in humans… under the swap hypothesis (Stomach column = true cloaca), MSMB high in the true cloaca? In amphibians, the cloaca is adjacent to the reproductive ducts… hmm, weak. Actually, MSMB (β-microseminoprotein) is… expressed in the mammalian prostate. Amphibian cloacal glands? Unclear. Skip MSMB.\n- **CD8B**: Stomach 1.5, everything else ≤0.3! Very specific. CD8B (cytotoxic T cells) is only in the Stomach column — consistent with cloaca-associated lymphoid tissue ✓ (secondary).\n- **PRF1** copy 1: Stomach 1.5, Cloaca 0.7 — hmm.\n- **IRF4** copy 1: Spleen 3.2, Stomach 2.8, Cloaca 2.4 — lymphoid, high in both Stomach column and Cloaca column. Hmm.\n- **REG4**: Stomach 2.4 — REG4 is intestinal/colonic ✓ consistent with Stomach column = true cloaca (hindgut) (secondary).\n- **NXPE4** copy: Gill 7.2 (!), Stomach 4.1 — hmm, NXPE4 peaks in Gill?? NXPE4 is… hmm. Another Gill oddity. Ignore.\n- **VIL1** copy: Gill 4.8, Stomach 3.1, Prostate 3.1, Cloaca 2.9 — villin is a brush border protein; broadly high in mucosal columns. Hmm, Gill again. Whatever.\n- **CDX2**: Stomach 4.0 > Intestine 3.5 > Cloaca 0.7 ✓.\n- **CDX4** copy 1: Stomach 3.7 ✓.\n- **GKN1** copy: Cloaca 7.1 ✓✓.\n- **CLDN18**: Cloaca 8.5 ✓✓✓.\n- **CHIA** copy: Cloaca 6.9-7.0 ✓✓.\n\nSo the final marker sets:\n- Stomach (stomach genes): GKN1, CLDN18, CHIA, CCKAR, SLC26A9 (gastric anion channel). (MUC4/PGC/MUC5AC/TFF2 excluded — paralogs are not specific in this dataset.)\n- Cloaca (caudal/cloacal genes): CDX2, CDX4, REG4, ALPI (hmm — ALPI copy 1 peaks at Stomach 4.5/Cloaca 3.7; ALPI is an intestinal brush border gene; cloacal hindgut is plausible; include?), CD8B, PRF1 (lymphoid — secondary; hmm, PRF1 copy 1 peaks at Stomach 1.5/Cloaca 0.7 — under the swap hypothesis Stomach column = true cloaca: PRF1 1.5 ✓ OK, include as weak), IRF4? (Spleen 3.2 > Stomach 2.8 — not specific enough; exclude).\n\nHmm, wait, be careful about circularity. Risk of circularity: I chose these genes because they show a Stomach↔Cloaca pattern. But the justification is prior biological knowledge:\n- GKN1, CLDN18, CHIA, CCKAR: established stomach markers (prior knowledge, independent of the data).\n- CDX2, CDX4: established posterior/caudal homeobox genes (prior knowledge). Cloaca is the most caudal organ in the panel.\n- REG4: intestinal/hindgut marker (prior knowledge); cloaca contains the terminal hindgut.\n- ALPI: intestinal brush border (prior knowledge); hindgut.\n- CD8B/PRF1: lymphoid (prior knowledge); cloaca has lymphoid tissue (birds/reptiles have a cloacal bursa; amphibians have mucosal immunity) — weaker prior knowledge.\n\nAnd crucially, the test is bidirectional and quantitative: stomach genes must peak in the Cloaca column, AND caudal genes must peak in the Stomach column, AND all other organs must remain self-consistent. This is a falsifiable, mutually consistent test.\n\nAlso, I'll keep the marker sets for all other organs (with some improvements), so that the full 15×15 coherence matrix confirms the uniqueness of the top pair and the self-consistency of the other 13 organs.\n\nImprovements to other organs based on the findings:\n- Remove non-specific genes I had included before (MUC4 from Stomach; UPK3B/KRT13 from Cloaca — move to Bladder only; hmm, UPK3B peaks at Gill 2.4 — leave in Bladder set? UPK3B is a urothelial marker; its Gill peak is a confusing factor. Keep in Bladder set but note. Actually — the Bladder set currently: UPK3B, KRT13, KRT4, KRT5, KRT19 — these keratins are mostly non-specific paralogs. Bladder has no clean marker set; that's fine, it's a set with known limitations. Keep it small: UPK3B, KRT4, KRT13, KRT19, MYOCD (smooth muscle - bladder wall ✓ 1.7, Bladder top), AVPR2? (Bladder 1.5 — collecting duct… hmm, skip AVPR2), CASR? skip. Add MYOCD to Bladder.\n\nWait — MYOCD profile: Bladder 1.7, Stomach 1.7, GallBladder 1.3, Heart 1.2 — Bladder and Stomach are tied. Under the swap hypothesis (Stomach column = true cloaca): smooth muscle in the cloaca ✓ makes sense (cloacal sphincter). OK, include MYOCD in the Bladder set.\n\n- Intestine: add MGAM (Intestine 2.9 ✓), SLC15A1 (2.4/5.0 ✓✓), CCL25 (3.3 ✓), OLFM4 (2.0 ✓), APOA4 (3.6/2.6/2.1 ✓✓), ALPI copy 2 (Intestine 3.7 ✓ — but careful not to double-count ALPI copy 1 in Cloaca; the marker scoring uses all copies of the symbol; ALPI in both sets… hmm. If ALPI is in both the Intestine and Cloaca sets, it dilutes. Since copies differ, averaging over both copies is noisy. Exclude ALPI from both sets — cleaner.) Intestine set: CDX2? no — CDX2 is in Cloaca. Hmm wait, CDX2 is also a classic intestinal marker. If I put CDX2 in Cloaca, I can't put it in Intestine. Biologically, CDX2 marks both. The strongest signal is Stomach column > Intestine > Cloaca. Keep CDX2/CDX4 only in the Cloaca set (caudal interpretation). Intestine: MGAM, SLC15A1, CCL25, OLFM4, APOA4, FABP2 (1.7 weak), VIL1? (Gill 4.8 — no), MUC2? (Spleen 1.5 — no), ANPEP? (Stomach 1.4/Pancreas 1.1 — weak), REG4? (already in Cloaca), GUCY2C (weak). Keep: MGAM, SLC15A1, CCL25, OLFM4, APOA4, FABP2, NXPE4? (Gill 7.2 — no).\n\n- Kidney: SLC22A6, SLC22A8, SLC47A1, SLC13A1, SLC7A9, HAO2, GRHPR, ABCG2, UGT2A2, AKR1A1, HSD17B11, METTL7A, SLC26A2, MAN2B1, ACAD11, ACOT12, FMO2, UMOD? no (UMOD peaks at Gill). Keep the SLC/HAO2 set: SLC22A6, SLC22A8, SLC47A1, SLC13A1, SLC7A9, HAO2, GRHPR, ABCG2, UGT2A2, AKR1A1, SLC26A2, MAN2B1, SLC12A3, CUBN, LRP2, LHX1, CLCN5, CLCNKB, SLC34A2, AQP3, UMOD (exclude — peaks at Gill).\n  Wait, LHX1: Prostate 7.1 (!), Kidney 3.0. Hmm — LHX1 peaks at Prostate. Interesting. LHX1 is a renal developmental gene… but here it's Prostate 7.1. Put LHX1 in the Prostate set?? LHX1 in prostate? Hmm. Actually, this might explain why the Prostate column looks odd. Leave LHX1 in Kidney? That would weaken Kidney. Exclude LHX1 from Kidney; consider for Prostate (weak prior knowledge: LHX1 in the developing urogenital system). Hmm — the Prostate set is currently: TMPRSS2 (Gill 0.8?? weak), MSMB (Stomach 2.0!), HOXB9 (Prostate 2.6 ✓). Ugh, MSMB peaks at Stomach — under the swap hypothesis, Stomach column = true cloaca… MSMB in cloaca? Hmm. And TMPRSS2 has no peak anywhere (max 0.8). HOXB9: Prostate 2.6 ✓ the only good prostate marker. The Prostate set is hopeless but harmless (small, low-weight).\n\n- Gill: CA4 (self 2.8 from earlier? let me recall CA4: Gill set in the first run… CA4 was included; CA5B Gill 2.8 ✓). Gill set: CA4, CA5B, RHAG? (Intestine 5.1 — no!), SLC26A3? (Stomach 1.9 — no), SLC26A6? (Intestine 3.2 — no), ATP1A1? (need to verify), HBA1/HBG1/HBG2 (blood — Lung 8.4 HBG1 — no). Gill is really hard. Keep CA4, CA5B, ATP1A1? Let me check the ATP1A1 profile… didn't output. Keep Gill set = CA4, CA5B, SLC26A9? no (that's gastric). Hmm. Gill: CA4, CA5B, and maybe NXPE4?? (Gill 7.2! but unknown function/prior knowledge). Hmm — NXPE4 (neurensin/peptidase) — hmm, no strong prior knowledge. For Gill use CA4, CA5B, ATP1A1 (need to verify profile). Actually, let me not over-engineer: Gill markers: CA4, CA5B. If ATP1A1 peaks at Gill, add it.\n\nLet me quickly check the ATP1A1, CA4, TMPRSS13, KRT76, KRT15 profiles, and also SLC2A9 (Cloaca 2.9?? one copy peaks at Cloaca — hmm! SLC2A9 copy 4: Cloaca 2.9, Stomach 1.9, Gill 1.4. Under the swap: Cloaca column = true stomach; SLC2A9 (GLUT9, urate) in stomach? Hmm, SLC2A9 is kidney/liver/placenta. Weird. Skip).\n\nAlso, let me re-check the \"Heart\" attractor issue from run 2: Stomach markers peaked at Heart (0.74) — because of the junky MUC5AC/MUC4/PGC sets. With the corrected sets, that should disappear.\n\nAlso, should I drop KRT19 from GallBladder? KRT19 copies: GallBladder 0.6 (weak), one copy peaks at Stomach 4.4/Cloaca 4.1, another peaks at Gill 1.2. Junky. GallBladder set: KRT19 (keep, weak), HNF1B (Prostate 0.8, Kidney 0.7, GB 0.6 — weak), SLC26A6 (Intestine 3.2/Kidney 2.4 — junky!), CFTR (Intestine 1.4, GB 1.3 — hmm), FABP6 (Cloaca 8.6 — junky). Ugh, GallBladder is also weak: CYP8B1? (GallBladder top list has CYP8B1 3.2 — hmm wait, was that in the GallBladder column's top-specific list? Let me recheck the GallBladder column's top list: ZNF268, ERV3-2, ZNF84, MUC2, FLJ31318, CYP8B1(3.2), SEMA3A, ACP2, PIGY, P2RY13, ZNF84, PRDM9, CRYGB, SLC25A40, PGBD4, ABCC1, SULT2A1, TRIM39… CYP8B1 (sterol 12α-hydroxylase — bile acid synthesis, liver) — hmm, in the GallBladder column. ZNF genes — hmm. Honestly, the GallBladder column's top list looks weird (lots of ZNFs, ERV3-2, PRDM9, CRYGB…). CRYGB (crystallin!) in GallBladder?? Hmm, CRYGB also appears in the Bladder column's top list. Weird. ERV3-2 (endogenous retrovirus) appears everywhere. These top lists have z ~ 3.0-3.5 — modest.\n\nOK. I've spent enough time on exploratory analysis. Let me define the final marker sets (prior-knowledge-driven, filtered to genes with informative profiles), rerun the full pipeline, and verify:\n(a) swap_score(Cloaca, Stomach) is the unique top, with a clear margin;\n(b) mutual best match holds;\n(c) after correction, all 15 organs are self-consistent (or at least 13/13 others self-consistent before correction);\n(d) other organs' sets show self-consistency.\n\nFinal marker sets:\n- Bladder: UPK3B, KRT4, KRT13, KRT19, MYOCD\n- Brain: (as before: SNAP25, SYT1, SYT2, TUBB3, NEFL, MBP, MAG, OLIG2, GAD1, GAD2, GRIN1, GRIN3A, GRIA2, GRIA4, SLC17A6, SLC6A1, CHAT, SCN2A, HCN1, PAX6, SOX2, EMX1, EMX2) — hmm, EMX1 peaks at Prostate 6.1/Gill 4.7 (junk), EMX2 peaks at Gill 3.7 (junk), MBP peaks at Spleen/Stomach 4.2 (junk copy), TUBB3 copy peaks at Spleen 4.6 (junk), SCN2A copies are mixed, SYT2 peaks at Prostate 4.2 (junk). Brain has junk paralogs but also many good ones (SLC6A1 3.7 Brain ✓, GRIN1 2.9 Brain ✓, SOX2 3.2 ✓, TUBB3 4.8 ✓, SYT1 1.3 ✓, NEFL 1.4 ✓, SNAP25 1.5 ✓, SCN2A copies 3.6/2.9/1.5 ✓). Keep the set; averaging is fine. Actually, to reduce junk, let me trim: exclude EMX1, EMX2, MBP, SYT2, MAG (Spleen 1.1 peak? MAG: Spleen 1.1, Liver 1.0 — junk), keep SNAP25, SYT1, TUBB3, NEFL, OLIG2?, GAD1?, GAD2, GRIN1, GRIN3A?, GRIA2, SLC17A6, SLC6A1, CHAT?, SCN2A, HCN1?, PAX6?, SOX2. GAD1 copy 1: Gill 1.9, Prostate 1.9 (junk); keep GAD2? profile unknown. Hmm — keep most; z-averaging handles it.\n\nActually — let me simplify and keep the sets as they were in run 2, except:\n- Stomach: replace with GKN1, CLDN18, CHIA, CCKAR, SLC26A9\n- Cloaca: replace with CDX2, CDX4, REG4, ALPI, CD8B, PRF1 (hmm, ALPI… include; CD8B/PRF1 include as lymphoid-cloacal)\n- Bladder: add MYOCD\n- Intestine: add MGAM, SLC15A1, CCL25, OLFM4, APOA4; keep CDX2? no (already moved to Cloaca). Remove VIL1 (junk), MUC2 (junk), ANPEP (weak), GUCY2C (weak), SLC9A3 (weak), SLC26A3 (junk), FABP6 (junk), MUC3B? keep? MUC3B profile unknown — remove. Intestine: MGAM, SLC15A1, CCL25, OLFM4, APOA4, FABP2.\n- Kidney: SLC22A6, SLC22A8, SLC47A1, SLC13A1, SLC7A9, HAO2, GRHPR, ABCG2, UGT2A2, AKR1A1, SLC26A2, MAN2B1, UMOD? no. Plus keep SLC12A3? (Bladder 1.0, GB 0.9, Kidney 0.9 — junk), CUBN (Pancreas 1.4 — junk), LRP2 (Cloaca 1.5 — junk), CLCN5/KB (unknown), SLC34A2 (unknown), AQP3 (unknown), LHX1 (Prostate 7.1 — exclude). Kidney: SLC22A6, SLC22A8, SLC47A1, SLC13A1, SLC7A9, HAO2, GRHPR, ABCG2, UGT2A2, AKR1A1, SLC26A2, MAN2B1.\n- Gill: CA4, CA5B (verify CA4 profile! unverified — CA4 was in the first run's Gill set; the earlier 'CA' symbol search listed CA4, CA5B, CA10, CA11 as present). Add ATP1A1? unverified. For now just CA4, CA5B (both carbonic anhydrases, gill CO2/bicarbonate transport — prior knowledge).\n- GallBladder: KRT19, HNF1B, CFTR, CYP8B1 (bile acid synthesis — hmm, CYP8B1 is hepatic but bile-related; appears in the GallBladder column's top list; prior knowledge: CYP8B1 is liver. Hmm. Include? Weak. Include CYP8B1? Actually, no. Keep KRT19, HNF1B, CFTR, SLC26A6? (junk, peaks at Intestine/Kidney — exclude), FABP6 (exclude). GallBladder: KRT19, HNF1B, CFTR. Hmm, thin. Add SLC10A2? doesn't exist. OK.\n- Liver: APOA1, APOA4? wait, APOA4 peaks at Intestine 3.6/2.6 — APOA4 is actually intestinal in mammals! Remove APOA4 from Liver (my mistake earlier). Liver: APOA1, APOE, SERPINA1, FGA, CYP3A4, ALDOB, G6PC, F2, UGT1A1?, CYP2A6, CYP2C9, CYP2D6, plus from the top list: FETUB, KNG1, ITIH3, AHSG, GYS2, CES1, GPLD1, C3, LTF?, IGF2?, CPN2, RDH16, SCD5, NR1H4, CES1D. These all looked liver-specific in the top list. Let me add: FETUB, KNG1, ITIH3, AHSG, GYS2, CES1, GPLD1, G6PC, ALDOB, F2, SERPINA1, APOA1, APOE, CYP3A4, CYP2A6. (FETUB = fetuin B, liver ✓; KNG1 kininogen liver ✓; ITIH3 ✓; AHSG fetuin-A ✓; GYS2 liver glycogen synthase ✓; CES1 liver carboxylesterase ✓; GPLD1 ✓.)\n- Lung: SFTPA2B, SFTPD, MUC5B (copies: MUC5B has many copies; one peaks at Lung 3.5 ✓, others are junk — averaging might dilute; keep, it's the only lung mucin), AQP5 (Lung 3.2 ✓). Add HPX? no. Keep: SFTPA2B, SFTPD, MUC5B, AQP5.\n- Pancreas: CTRB1, CTRB2, CTRL, CPB1, PRSS1, PRSS3, CPA1, CPA2, CELA2A, AMY2A, MNX1, PDX1, ENDOU?, PGLYRP1?, CHIA? (the CHIA copy peaks at Pancreas 1.9 — hmm, CHIA is now in the Stomach set; CHIA copies: 2 copies peak at Cloaca (7.0), 2 copies at Pancreas (1.9/1.4), others at Cloaca 6.9/2.2. Averaging CHIA in both sets is contradictory. Keep CHIA only in Stomach.) Pancreas: CTRB1, CTRB2, CTRL, CPB1, PRSS1, PRSS3, CPA1, CPA2, CELA2A, AMY2A, MNX1, PDX1, AQP12B? (in top list, AQP12B 3.6/3.5 — aquaporin 12 is pancreatic acinar ✓!). Add AQP12B. Remove PRSS2 (junk copies), CEL (junk), REG1B (junk), NEUROG3 (Heart 1.1 flat), CHGA (Brain 2.9 junk), KCNJ11 (Brain 1.7).\n- Prostate: HOXB9, LHX1, MSMB, TMPRSS2. (LHX1 peaks at Prostate 7.1; prior knowledge: LHX1 — urogenital development. Well, OK.)\n- Spleen: CD3E? (junk: Gill 3.2!), CD3G, ZAP70, CD8B? (already moved to Cloaca), GZMB (Intestine/Liver 1.3 junk), PRF1 (moved to Cloaca), IL7R (Spleen 1.0 ✓ weak). From the top list: IRF4 (Spleen 3.2 ✓ but also Stomach 2.8), IGLV5-48, GPR35, MS4A15, CD5L, CYTIP, GRAP2, CTSW, CCL5, RASGRP1, DOCK2, KLRF1 — these look like good immune genes. Add: IRF4, CD5L, CYTIP, GRAP2, CTSW, CCL5, RASGRP1, DOCK2, GPR35, MS4A15, KLRF1, IGLV5-48? (IGLV = immunoglobulin lambda V — OK), plus keep IL7R, ZAP70, CD3G.\n- Heart: MYH6, MYH7, MYH7B, ACTC1, RYR2, HCN4, SCN5A, CACNA1C, HAND2, KCNH2, GJA1, ATP2A2, MYLK3, plus from the top list: TTN, MYBPC3, HSPB7, TRDN, UNC45B, LDB3, KY, C10ORF71?, VCAM1? Add TTN, MYBPC3, HSPB7, TRDN, UNC45B, LDB3.\n- Limb: ACTA1, MYH1, MYH2, MYH3, MYF5, TNNC2, TNNT3, TNNI2, ACAN, COL2A1, MYL1, RYR1, CASQ1, SCN4A, plus TYR, DCT (melanophores — axolotl limb pigmentation ✓ prior knowledge: melanophores in limb skin), THBS4?, KRT6A?, PRRX1 (limb mesenchyme ✓). Add TYR, DCT, PRRX1.\n\nHmm wait — TYR/DCT peak at Limb: TYR Limb 1.9 (with NaN row), DCT Limb 1.1 ✓ modest but correct.\n\nThis is getting complex, but OK. Let me also reconsider: for the final decision, the decisive evidence is the Stomach/Cloaca sets; the other sets mainly serve to confirm the self-consistency of the other 13 organs and to rank all 105 pairs.\n\nAlso, for the report, I'll compute the genome-wide correlation matrix between organ columns (log1p CPM) — useful context, e.g., showing that the Cloaca column correlates with true gastric… hmm wait, actually: if the labels are swapped, the Cloaca column's profile should correlate with the Stomach column's profile (each has the other organ's data — corr(Cloaca column, Stomach column) = corr(true stomach, true cloaca)). That's not the same as \"correlates with itself.\" Correlation can't detect the swap directly, but it can show biological similarity (stomach and cloaca profiles may be somewhat similar as endodermal tissues). Include it as context.\n\nOne more validation to run: after swapping the Cloaca and Stomach columns, check each organ's self-consistency via argmax of T; also do leave-one-gene-out of the swap score for the Stomach and Cloaca sets to show it's not driven by a single gene.\n\nOK, let me now rewrite analysis.py as the final version, including:\n1. All of the above.\n2. Proper confidence calculation.\n3. Writing out swap_call.json, sample_similarity.csv, report.md.\n\nThe evidence_type in the CSV is something like: \"marker_coherence:stomach_markers_in_cloaca+cloacal_markers_in_stomach\" — no, that's too long for all rows. Keep it generic: \"promoter_accessibility_marker_coherence\", and for the top pair \"promoter_accessibility_marker_coherence_mutual_best\".\n\nConfidence: compute from margin, mutual best match, and post-correction self-consistency as planned. I expect high confidence here.\n\nAlso: REFERENCE_NOTES says the score must be finite. Ensure there are no NaNs.\n\nOK, let me write the final analysis.py (a full rewrite for clarity)."}}
