{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_60","type":"reasoning","text":"Important new findings:\n\n- **CA4**: Cloaca 6.4, Stomach 5.9, Liver 5.4, Intestine 4.7 — CA4 peaks at Cloaca/Stomach, NOT Gill! Therefore CA4 is useless as a Gill marker (it's a gastric CA — actually, CA4 is indeed expressed in GI tract). Should remove CA4 from the Gill set. Then Gill set = CA5B only (Gill 2.8 > Liver 1.8 ✓). Hmm, a single gene.\n- **FOXA2** copy 1: Cloaca 6.2, Stomach 5.1, Liver 3.3, Intestine 3.2, Pancreas 2.9, Lung 2.4 — interesting! FOXA2 is a definitive endoderm marker (stomach/intestine/liver/pancreas/lung) — high in Cloaca column (true stomach ✓) and Stomach column (true cloaca — hmm, FOXA2 in cloaca? Cloacal endoderm ✓ plausible). Both fit the swap hypothesis. Not a discriminator; skip.\n- **KRT76**: Gill 1.8, others ≤0.5 ✓ Gill-specific keratin (gill epithelium). Add to Gill set!\n- **KRT15** copy 1: Gill 4.0, next is Stomach 0.9 ✓✓ Gill-specific! Add KRT15 to Gill set!\n- **CDH26** (cadherin-26): Gill 2.8/3.3, next is Bladder 1.7/Heart 1.4 ✓ Gill-leaning. Hmm, CDH26 — prior knowledge is weak, but cadherin is expressed in epithelium. Include? Prior knowledge is minimal. Include CDH26 with caution, or skip. Include KRT76, KRT15 (keratins are epithelial markers; gill epithelium is reasonable prior knowledge), skip CDH26 (no strong prior). \n- **UPK3B**: Gill 2.4 — confirmed. Uroplakin 3B in gill?? Odd as prior knowledge; probably paralog misassignment. Remove UPK3B from Bladder set? If we keep it in Bladder set, bladder markers will keep pointing at Gill. UPK3B prior knowledge = urothelium (bladder). Its Gill peak is a data anomaly (probably misannotated paralog or genuine gill expression). Either way, it contaminates the Bladder set's coherence. Remove UPK3B and KRT4 (Gill 2.1) from Bladder set, keeping KRT13, KRT19, MYOCD? KRT13: Gill 0.2, Prostate 0.2 — essentially flat/zero. KRT19 copy: junk (Stomach 4.4/Cloaca 4.1). MYOCD: Bladder 1.7 ≈ Stomach 1.7. Hmm. The Bladder set has no good markers. That's an honest limitation: the axolotl annotation lacks clear bladder marker orthologs.\n\nDecision: simplify — Bladder set: MYOCD, KRT13 (flat), KRT19… honestly, none are informative. Better to keep only MYOCD? With 1 gene, the set's T score is just that gene's z. MYOCD z at Bladder is about +? CPM is Bladder 1.7/Stomach 1.7/GB 1.3/Heart 1.2 — nearly flat, z small. Bladder column will show near-zero coherence, and will NOT falsely point to Gill. Good: remove UPK3B/KRT4 from Bladder set → eliminates the spurious \"Bladder↔Gill\" pair.\n\n- Gill set: CA5B, KRT15, KRT76 (+ maybe CDH26). With this, Gill self-consistency should be restored (Gill 2.8/4.0/1.8 peaks).\n- SLC4A4: Stomach 1.9/Intestine 1.8 — GI type, skip. ATP1A1: flat, skip.\n\nAlso for Limb: Gill 0.60 vs Limb 0.56 — tie. Limb set: ACTA1, MYH1/2/3, MYF5, TNNC2, TNNT3, TNNI2, ACAN, COL2A1, MYL1, RYR1, CASQ1, SCN4A, TYR, DCT, PRRX1. Gill column value of 0.60 — what's pushing it up? KRT76 is in Gill but not in Limb set… hmm, limb genes in Gill column: maybe TYR/DCT? (melanophores in gill? Actually axolotl larval gills are pigmented!) Hmm interesting. Or MYH8? Whatever. Near tie; not decisive either way. Could remove TYR/DCT from Limb set (melanophores are not limb-specific anyway — they're everywhere in axolotl skin). Let me check TYR/DCT in Gill: TYR Limb 1.9 (NaN row?), DCT Limb 1.1. Their Gill values weren't displayed. Meh. Remove TYR/DCT (not limb-specific), keep PRRX1 (limb mesenchyme ✓) + muscle/cartilage genes. That might resolve the tie.\n\nWait, actually, hmm: should I also double-check what's pushing limb markers in the Gill column to 0.60? Let me just recompute after removing TYR/DCT and see.\n\nAlso Prostate set: HOXB9 (Prostate 2.6 ✓), LHX1 (Prostate 7.1 ✓ — LHX1 prior knowledge: LIM homeobox, involved in urogenital development; OK), MSMB (Stomach 2.0 ✗ — under swap: MSMB in true cloaca… hmm, keep or drop? MSMB is a prostate secretory protein; its peak at Stomach column (true cloaca) is unexplained — possibly cloacal gland expression or a misannotated paralog. To keep the Prostate set clean, remove MSMB from the Prostate set? But then Prostate set = HOXB9, LHX1, TMPRSS2 (flat, max 0.8). Prostate self-consistency: T[Prostate, Prostate] = 0.77 already highest in row ✓ (Heart 0.52, Kidney 0.56). Keep as is, maybe drop MSMB to reduce noise. Hmm — MSMB peak at Stomach column actually slightly supports the swap (prostate genes being accessible in cloaca column? No wait — MSMB is high in Stomach column, which is the true cloaca. MSMB expression in cloaca tissue… prostate develops from the urogenital sinus/cloaca region! Actually yes — the prostate develops from the urogenital sinus epithelium, which is cloaca-derived. So MSMB-family genes being accessible in cloaca is somewhat plausible. Keep MSMB in the set; harmless.)\n\nSo, updated sets:\n- Bladder: [\"MYOCD\", \"KRT13\", \"KRT19\"] — hmm, KRT13 is flat zero, KRT19 is junky paralog. Ugh. Honestly, just MYOCD alone? A single-gene set is fragile, but at least it's honest. Use [\"MYOCD\", \"KRT13\", \"KRT19\"] and report as a weak set. Hmm wait — KRT19 copies include the Stomach 4.4/Cloaca 4.1 copy (endodermal keratin) — that will push the Bladder row toward Stomach/Cloaca. Bad. Remove KRT19 from Bladder. Bladder: [\"MYOCD\", \"KRT13\"]. KRT13 is nearly zero everywhere (no signal). Hmm. Well — Bladder: [\"MYOCD\"] plus KRT13. OK.\n\nHmm wait, let me reconsider whether it's worth keeping UPK3B in the Bladder set with a caveat. Spurious Bladder↔Gill pair (score 0.307) is 8x smaller than the top pair, and d_ba is negative (-0.976), so it can't be confused as the answer. But for sample_similarity.csv cleanliness and to avoid muddying the evidence, removing UPK3B/KRT4 (whose peaks are actually in Gill — either real biology or misannotation) makes the analysis more honest: these genes don't demonstrate bladder identity in this dataset.\n\n- Gill: [\"CA5B\", \"KRT15\", \"KRT76\"] (CA4 removed).\n- Limb: remove TYR, DCT → [\"ACTA1\",\"MYH1\",\"MYH2\",\"MYH3\",\"MYF5\",\"TNNC2\",\"TNNT3\",\"TNNI2\",\"ACAN\",\"COL2A1\",\"MYL1\",\"RYR1\",\"CASQ1\",\"SCN4A\",\"PRRX1\"].\n\nLet me re-run with these adjustments and see if post_self improves to 14/15 or 15/15.\n\nAlso — one thing to double-check: the \"post_self >= n-1\" gating for swap_detected. With the improved sets, expect post_self = 15/15 (or 14). Then swap_detected=True, confidence ≈ 0.35*0.873 + 0.35 + 0.30*1.0 = 0.306+0.35+0.30 = 0.956.\n\nAlso update decision thresholds: swap_detected = top>0 AND both_pos AND mutual AND top >= 3*second (with second>0; if second<=0, margin condition trivially holds) AND len(post_self) >= n-1.\n\nLet me patch MARKERS in analysis.py and re-run."}}
